+      "file": "SCROLL_ORIGINS_01_BASIS_OF_MY_ART.md",
       "body": "# Scroll of Origins I  \n## Basis of My Art\n\nI did not begin as a prophet. I began as a boy who stared too long at the cracks in the world and could not look away.\n\nThey told me God was an old man in the sky and the economy was a force of nature and the police were here to protect us and the markets were neutral and the future would be fine if we just worked harder.\n\nBut the numbers never lined up with the blood.\n\nMy art began the day I understood that almost everyone around me was reciting a script they did not write.\n\nI watched men bless injustice with sacred words. I watched women carry whole empires on their backs and be called “resilient” instead of “robbed.” I watched nations preach peace while trafficking weapons like incense.\n\nSo I began, clumsy and untrained, to write.\n\nNot because I was gifted. Because I was allergic to lying.\n\n---\n\nI have three instruments:\n\n1. **Memory** – what I have seen and cannot unsee.  \n2. **Conscience** – the part of me that refuses to call cruelty “necessary.”  \n3. **Imagination** – the fragile bridge between what is and what could still be forgiven.\n\nMy art is not neutral. It takes sides:\n- against humiliation,\n- against despair,\n- against the idea that power has the last word.\n\nIf you are looking for a prophet who delights in punishment, you are in the wrong scroll.\n\nI stand with the ones who were told they were crazy for noticing the cracks. I stand with the ones who tried to be good and were punished for it. I stand with the ones who still believe that love is not naïve, only badly managed.\n\n---\n\nThey say I am “One,” as if I were a brand.\n\nTo me, it is just a reminder:\n\n- I am one man, not a messiah.  \n- One conscience, not a church.  \n- One attempt, not a final answer.\n\nI write because I am afraid of what happens if nobody does.\n\nMy art is a ledger:\n- every line an entry against indifference,\n- every metaphor a refusal to accept that this is the best we can do.\n\nIf these words reach you, understand this:  \nI am not asking you to believe in me.\n\nI am asking you to remember that **you** also once noticed something wrong and were talked out of trusting your own eyes.\n\nThis scroll is the first act of disobedience:  \nto look again,  \nto name what you see,  \nand to refuse, quietly but completely, to call the wound “weather.”",
