-      "body": "Scroll of Origins XI\n\nThe Boy Who Believed\n\nBefore they called him dangerous,\nbefore they called him arrogant,\nbefore they called him prophet,\nthere was just a boy in a too-big uniform\nsitting under a crucifix he took too seriously.\n\nThe nuns said,\n“You must try to be like Jesus.”\nThey meant it as morale,\nas routine instruction,\nthe kind of thing you say to keep children soft and manageable.\n\nHe didn’t hear it that way.\n\nHe heard an assignment.\n\nHe heard it like a contract written in chalk and blood:\nIf that is the highest thing a man can be,\nthen what else is there to aim for?\n\nSo while other boys thought about games and girlfriends and getting out,\nhe sat in the back row of religion class,\nstaring at the Stations of the Cross\nand quietly asking himself\nwhere exactly a person was supposed to put their fear\nif they planned to live like that.\n\nHe was not the best student.\nHe was not the purest heart.\nHe was not even especially obedient.\n\nHe just believed them.\n\nThat was his first mistake.\n\t•\t\n\nCatholic school is its own small country.\n\nThe flag is a crucifix.\nThe anthem is guilt.\nThe border guards wear black and white and carry rosaries\nlike keys to a kingdom you’re never quite sure you deserve.\n\nHe watched how it worked.\n\nHow the rules bent for some families and not others.\nHow the “least of these” got quoted from the pulpit\nand ignored in the hallway.\nHow a God who supposedly washed feet\nwas represented by men who never bent down.\n\nSomething in him started taking notes long before he had language for it.\n\nHe didn’t know the word “hypocrisy” yet.\nHe just knew that the Jesus in the stories\ndid not behave like the Jesus in the building.\n\nAnd if he was supposed to be like Jesus,\nwhich Jesus did they mean?\n\nThe gentle one in the paintings,\nlit by candles and lilies?\nOr the one who flipped tables\nand stared power in the eye with no backup?\n\nThe boy felt both living in his chest\nand learned early that the second one\nmade adults very uncomfortable.\n\t•\t\n\nThere was a day — he will remember it until he dies —\nwhen a teacher, tired and impatient,\nhumiliated a poorer student in front of the whole class\nfor not having the right shoes.\n\nAfterward, they prayed aloud for “the poor.”\n\nThe boy’s stomach burned.\n\nHe didn’t have the words,\nbut his body knew something was wrong with that prayer.\n\nHe wondered if God accepted it,\nor sent it back marked insufficient honesty.\n\nThat night, lying in a hot, dark room,\nhe whispered into the ceiling:\n\n“If You’re real,\ndon’t let me become like that.\nIf I’m supposed to be like You,\ndon’t let them be the ones who define You.”\n\nIt was a child’s theology,\nbut it was clean.\n\nHe was not asking to be powerful.\nHe was asking not to be a liar.\n\nThat was the beginning of his allergy to lying:\nthe itch in his soul every time someone used holy words\nto decorate an unholy act.\n\nHe would never grow out of it.\nIt would cost him almost everything.\n\t•\t\n\nThey taught him that Jesus was both God and man.\n\nNo one prepared him for what it meant\nto try to be “like Jesus” when you are only man.\n\nHe carried it like a private dare.\n\nWhen he lost his temper,\nhe counted it as failure.\nWhen he lied to avoid trouble,\nhe felt he had betrayed a contract no one else remembered signing.\nWhen he looked at the world’s cruelty and felt nothing,\nhe worried his heart was hardening\ninto the same stone he saw in the eyes of the pious.\n\nThere were no medals for this.\nNo gold stars for excessive conscience.\n\nJust a growing, quiet distance between him\nand people who could say the creed on Sunday\nand then go back to normal on Monday\nas if they had not just promised everything.\n\nHe did not know it yet,\nbut he was already on his way out of religion\nand deeper into God.\n\t•\t\n\nLater, much later,\nthey would say he was obsessed,\ngrandiose,\ndelusional.\n\nThey would say he thought he was the Son of God.\n\nThat was never it.\n\nHe never thought he was Jesus.\n\nHe thought the adults meant what they said\nwhen they told a little boy\nthat the highest thing a man could be\nwas to live like Him.\n\nHe took that assignment seriously,\nand the world never forgave him for it.\n\nBecause a boy who believes he must live like that\ngrows into a man who will not sit quietly\nwhile power lies,\nwhile churches bless what should be condemned,\nwhile nations crucify and call it order.\n\nThey will call that madness.\nThey will call that rebellion.\nThey will call that heresy.\n\nHe still calls it\ndoing the homework they gave him in third grade.\n\t•\t\n\nIf you want to understand the prophet,\ndon’t start with the speeches\nor the manifestos\nor the presidential enemies list.\n\nStart with the child in the too-big uniform,\nstaring at a wooden man nailed to a wall,\nthinking:\n\n“If that’s the standard,\nthen half of you are lying\nand the other half are asleep.\n\nAnd if no one else is going to take this seriously,\nmaybe I have to.”\n\nThe boy did not know\nthat this vow would one day drag him\ninto politics,\ninto exile,\ninto love he could not keep,\ninto wars he did not want.\n\nHe only knew this:\n\nIf you tell a child\nto build their life around a story,\nyou should be very careful\nwhat story you hand them.\n\nSome of them will actually do it.\n\nAnd they will come back one day,\ngrown and furious and heartbroken,\nasking why the adults never meant it.\n\nThis is his answer.\n\nThis is where it began.\n\nThe boy who believed\nis still here.\n\nHe just has fewer illusions\nabout the people who taught him how.\n\n⸻",
