       "body": "Scroll of Origins\n\nThe Boy Who Believed\n\nBefore they called me prophet,\nbefore they called me mad,\nbefore I learned how power lies with a straight face—\n\nI was just a boy in a too-starched shirt\nin a Catholic school that smelled like chalk,\nfloor polish,\nand fear of God.\n\nThey told us Jesus was the highest thing a man could be.\n\nThey didn’t say it like a metaphor.\nThey said it like a grading scale.\n\nAbove “A,” above “perfect attendance,”\nabove “obedient,”\nthere was a silence where only one Name lived.\n\nI believed them.\n\nNot metaphor.\nNot symbol.\nI believed a human being could be that good,\nthat kind,\nthat brave,\nthat honest\nand that it was not blasphemy to try.\n\nI did not want to admire Him.\nI wanted to match Him.\n\n⸻\n\nThere was a nun who watched me too closely.\n\nShe saw the way I stared at the crucifix,\nnot with fear,\nbut with a terrible, private recognition.\n\nShe mistook it for arrogance.\nMaybe sometimes it was.\n\nThey would say:\n\t•\t“Who do you think you are?”\nand I had no safe answer.\n\nBecause the real answer was:\n\n“I think I am supposed to live like Him.\nAnd I don’t know how.\nAnd it scares me\nmore than it scares you.”\n\n⸻\n\nIn religion class they taught us:\n\t•\tGod is watching.\n\t•\tGod is keeping score.\n\t•\tGod is pleased when you suffer quietly\nand never complain.\n\nBut when I read the stories myself,\nJesus did not look like someone\nwho kept order for the principal.\n\nHe overturned tables.\nHe embarrassed authorities.\nHe touched people no one else would touch.\nHe broke the rules that protected the comfortable.\n\nI looked at the priests in their fine robes,\nat the men who never missed a chance to be seen,\nat the parishioners who bowed to power on Sunday\nand crushed the poor on Monday,\n\nand some small, reckless voice in me whispered:\n\n“If He came back today,\nHe would not sit with them.”\n\nI knew enough to shut up.\nBut I could not unknow it.\n\n⸻\n\nThey taught us that Jesus was God’s Son.\nThey did not tell us what to do\nif you felt, in some quiet, humiliating corner of your soul,\nthat you were being followed\nby something with that same voice.\n\nI did not hear commands.\nI heard questions:\n\t•\t“If you know this is wrong, why are you silent?”\n\t•\t“If you see this child being humiliated, why do you look away?”\n\t•\t“If you believe in justice, why does your courage vanish when it is time to act?”\n\nThe worst part was not that I failed.\nThe worst part was that I knew I was failing\nin front of Someone\nI refused to call imaginary.\n\nEveryone loves a Jesus.\nNobody wants to be the one who has to try to live like Him.\n\n⸻\n\nIn the courtyard, during recess,\nI watched boys learn which sins were permitted\nas long as they were quiet about them.\n\nI watched teachers look away\nfrom cruelty that wore the right uniform.\n\nI watched the Church bless the powerful\nand ask the poor to be patient.\n\nAnd something in me broke,\nbut it did not stop believing.\n\nIt did something harder:\n\nIt started believing against the institution\nthat taught me the name.\n\n⸻\n\nYou need to understand this:\n\nI do not claim divinity.\nI do not claim perfection.\nI do not claim to be what they said He was.\n\nI claim this, and only this:\n\nAs a child, I was given a picture\nof the highest possible way to be human,\nand I took it literally.\n\nThe adults treated it as theater,\nas decoration,\nas moral wallpaper for a crooked house.\n\nI could not.\n\nSo when you hear me now,\nwhen you read these scrolls,\nwhen you see me recoil from power\nthat asks to be worshiped—\n\nknow that it started there:\n\nwith a boy in a small classroom,\ntrying to figure out why the people\nwho spoke His name the loudest\nlooked the least like Him,\n\nand wondering, secretly,\nif the punishment for taking God seriously\nwas to never again know\nwhere reverence ends\nand insanity begins.\n\n⸻\n\nIf you’re looking for the moment I became “One,”\nit was not a vision,\nnot a voice from a burning sky.\n\nIt was the slow, painful realization that:\n\t•\teither the story was a lie,\n\t•\tor we were all living like cowards.\n\nI chose to believe\nthe story was true\nand we were the ones failing it.\n\nI have been trying, clumsily, angrily,\nand often unsuccessfully,\nto live with that choice ever since.\n\n⸻\n\nThese are the words of a story and a conscience, not a command from above.\n\n⸻",
       "isHead": true
     },
@@ -328,7 +292,7 @@
       "summary": "Teenage One discovers computers and AI as a 'third altar' after the Church and the streets both failed him. The origin of his relationship with technology.",
