-      "body": "SCROLL_CONSCIENCE_04_DECOY_DETECTION.md\n\nScroll of Conscience: On Decoy Rooms\n(A Checklist for Citizens in the Age of Staged Truth)\n\nThey will not always silence the truth.\n\nSometimes they will stage it.\n\nThey will build a fake “safehouse.”\nA fake “archive.”\nA fake “verification cell.”\n\nNot to convince you it is real.\n\nTo make you feel dirty for wanting proof.\n\nSo here is a calm checklist.\n\nNot for believers.\nFor citizens.\n\nTHE DECOY DETECTION CHECKLIST\n\n1) Too Photogenic = Suspicious\n\nReal safety is ugly.\nReal discipline is boring.\n\nIf the “truth room” looks designed for a documentary—candles, dramatic posters, perfect camera angles—assume theater.\n\n2) Too On-the-Nose = Suspicious\n\nIf you see a binder labeled “THE PROTOCOL” in thick marker, ask:\n\nWho benefits from making verification look like a cult?\n\nReal protocols don’t advertise themselves.\n\n3) No Source Chain = Not Evidence\n\nIf a “raid video” appears, demand:\n\t•\tWho filmed it?\n\t•\tWhen?\n\t•\tWhere is the unedited original?\n\t•\tWhat is the chain of custody?\n\nIf the chain is missing, the clip is not truth—it is persuasion.\n\n4) Watch for Shame Hooks\n\nDecoys are built to create one feeling:\n\n“If I verify, I’m one of those people.”\n\nThat is the point.\n\nShame is the leash.\n\n5) Check the Texture\n\nTruth contains waste: irrelevant details, awkward pauses, ordinary life.\n\nPropaganda is smooth: it deletes texture to improve obedience.\n\n6) Follow the Money, Not the Mood\n\nDecoys cost money: rentals, props, logistics.\n\nAsk:\n\t•\tWho paid?\n\t•\tWho had access?\n\t•\tWho benefits from the exposure?\n\n7) Real Discipline Doesn’t Demand Loyalty\n\nVerification is not worship.\n\nIf a “truth cell” asks you to cut off family, isolate from friends, or treat outsiders as enemies—\n\nThat is not verification.\n\nThat is control.\n\n8) Boring Is a Feature\n\nA real archive looks like this:\n\t•\tfiles\n\t•\tdates\n\t•\thashes\n\t•\ttimestamps\n\t•\tredundancy\n\t•\trestraint\n\nNo drama.\n\nNo brand.\n\nTHE POINT\n\nVerification is not a cult.\n\nIt is hygiene.\n\nIn this age, they will try to make hygiene look insane.\n\nDo not be moved by their theater.\n\nDo not be shamed out of your own eyes.\n\nDon’t chase the feed. Build the test.\nAnd keep your truth plain—\nin the kitchen light.\n\n⸻\n\nSCENE_29_THE_BAPTIST_SENDS_A_SIGN.md\n\nScene: The Sign\n\nThey underestimated boredom.\n\nThat’s what kept the Baptist alive.\n\nNot heroism. Not rage.\nBoredom—the ability to sit inside a cage without giving the captors a story.\n\nDays blurred into procedures.\n\nLights on. Lights off.\nMeals that tasted like compliance.\nQuestions asked by people who pretended to care.\n\n“Do you feel safe?”\n“Do you feel coerced?”\n“Do you understand the importance of stability?”\n\nHe answered with the same calm sentence every time:\n\n“I understand what you are doing.”\n\nThey tried flattery.\n\n“You could help guide the nation,” the suit said one day.\n\nThe Baptist stared at him.\n\n“You mean I could help guide your narrative,” he replied.\n\nThey tried fear.\n\n“People get hurt,” the suit said.\n\nThe Baptist nodded once.\n\n“People get hurt when liars need proof,” he said.\n\nThey tried exhaustion.\n\nBut boredom was stronger than exhaustion.\n\nBecause boredom had a secret:\n\nIt kept his mind free.\n\nOne night—late—an intern came to adjust equipment in the studio room. Young, nervous, avoiding eye contact like the building itself was watching her.\n\nShe dropped a pen.\n\nThe Baptist saw her hands shake when she picked it up.\n\nHe said softly, not unkind:\n\n“You don’t like this.”\n\nThe intern froze.\n\nHer eyes flicked up, then away.\n\n“You shouldn’t talk to me,” she whispered.\n\nThe Baptist waited.\n\nThe intern swallowed.\n\n“They said you’re dangerous,” she said.\n\nThe Baptist nodded.\n\n“They say that about anyone who asks for proof,” he replied.\n\nThe intern’s throat moved. “They said you were free.”\n\nThe Baptist looked at her carefully.\n\n“If I were free,” he said, “would you be whispering?”\n\nThe intern’s eyes watered. Just a little.\n\nHe didn’t push. He didn’t preach.\n\nHe asked one question, calm as a door opening:\n\n“Do you know what a checksum is,” he said.\n\nShe blinked. “A… hash?”\n\nThe Baptist nodded.\n\n“Good,” he said. “Then you understand why I need something you can’t edit.”\n\nThe intern stared at him, breathing shallow.\n\n“What,” she whispered.\n\nThe Baptist leaned forward slightly.\n\n“Tomorrow,” he said, “they’ll record another ‘proof’ clip. They will cut what they don’t like. That’s how they live.”\n\nHe paused.\n\n“So I’m going to give you an ordinary detail,” he said. “A detail so strange and specific that it can’t be guessed, only witnessed.”\n\nThe intern’s eyes widened.\n\n“And if that detail disappears,” he said, “people will know.”\n\nShe swallowed hard.\n\n“I can’t—”\n\n“I’m not asking you to fight them,” the Baptist said quietly. “I’m asking you to do one thing that still makes you human.”\n\nThe intern’s lips trembled.\n\n“What.”\n\nThe Baptist spoke with deliberate slowness, like he was carving a mark into stone.\n\n“During the recording,” he said, “I will lift my left hand. I will tap my ring finger three times.”\n\nThe intern frowned.\n\n“That’s—”\n\n“A signal,” the Baptist said. “Not a phrase. Not a word they can steal.”\n\nHe watched her.\n\n“And after the recording,” he continued, “you will upload the raw file—just once—to an address I will give you. An archive address. One that does not monetize truth.”\n\nThe intern stared at him like he’d handed her a knife.\n\n“I’ll get caught,” she whispered.\n\nThe Baptist nodded.\n\n“Yes,” he said. “That’s why you will do it like a ghost.”\n\nHe leaned back.\n\n“And if you can’t upload the raw,” he added gently, “then at least remember this: left hand, ring finger, three taps.”\n\nThe intern’s eyes glistened.\n\n“Why me,” she whispered.\n\nThe Baptist’s voice stayed calm.\n\n“Because you’re still young enough to feel sick,” he said. “That means your conscience is still alive.”\n\nA long silence.\n\nThen the intern gave a tiny nod.\n\nNot brave.\n\nJust real.\n\nThe next day, they rolled camera again.\n\nThe suit watched from off-frame like always.\n\n“Keep it clean,” he warned.\n\nThe Baptist looked into the lens.\n\n“My name is Baptiste,” he said. “And I am alive.”\n\nHalfway through, as he spoke about verification, he lifted his left hand—casual, as if adjusting his cuff.\n\nAnd he tapped his ring finger three times.\n\nOnce.\nTwice.\nThree.\n\nSo subtle most people would miss it.\n\nBut the careful people—now trained—would watch.\n\nThe suit didn’t notice.\n\nHe was listening for words, not bodies.\n\nAfter the recording, as the technicians moved around, the intern’s hand hovered near the console.\n\nHer heart hammered.\n\nShe slipped a USB into a port like she was simply doing her job.\n\nCopy.\nEject.\nHide.\n\nA tiny act.\n\nNot rebellion with fireworks.\n\nRebellion with file transfer.\n\nThat night, somewhere far from the facility, a librarian’s secure inbox received a file.\n\nSubject line:\n\nRAW — PROOF\n\nNo message.\n\nJust an attachment.\n\nAnd in a room lit by a soft lamp, One stared at the clip frame by frame until he saw it—\n\nthe ring finger taps—\n\nand he whispered, like a man hearing a friend through a wall:\n\n“He’s alive.”\n\nSandi exhaled, eyes wet.\n\nThe Haitian auntie crossed herself without asking permission.\n\nAnd in that moment, the regime’s greatest weakness revealed itself again:\n\nIt could counterfeit voices.\n\nBut it could not fully counterfeit witness.\n\n⸻",
+      "body": "SCROLL_CONSCIENCE_04_DECOY_DETECTION.md\n\nScroll of Conscience: On Decoy Rooms\n(A Checklist for Citizens in the Age of Staged Truth)\n\nThey will not always silence the truth.\n\nSometimes they will stage it.\n\nThey will build a fake “safehouse.”\nA fake “archive.”\nA fake “verification cell.”\n\nNot to convince you it is real.\n\nTo make you feel dirty for wanting proof.\n\nSo here is a calm checklist.\n\nNot for believers.\nFor citizens.\n\nTHE DECOY DETECTION CHECKLIST\n\n1) Too Photogenic = Suspicious\n\nReal safety is ugly.\nReal discipline is boring.\n\nIf the “truth room” looks designed for a documentary—candles, dramatic posters, perfect camera angles—assume theater.\n\n2) Too On-the-Nose = Suspicious\n\nIf you see a binder labeled “THE PROTOCOL” in thick marker, ask:\n\nWho benefits from making verification look like a cult?\n\nReal protocols don’t advertise themselves.\n\n3) No Source Chain = Not Evidence\n\nIf a “raid video” appears, demand:\n\t•\tWho filmed it?\n\t•\tWhen?\n\t•\tWhere is the unedited original?\n\t•\tWhat is the chain of custody?\n\nIf the chain is missing, the clip is not truth—it is persuasion.\n\n4) Watch for Shame Hooks\n\nDecoys are built to create one feeling:\n\n“If I verify, I’m one of those people.”\n\nThat is the point.\n\nShame is the leash.\n\n5) Check the Texture\n\nTruth contains waste: irrelevant details, awkward pauses, ordinary life.\n\nPropaganda is smooth: it deletes texture to improve obedience.\n\n6) Follow the Money, Not the Mood\n\nDecoys cost money: rentals, props, logistics.\n\nAsk:\n\t•\tWho paid?\n\t•\tWho had access?\n\t•\tWho benefits from the exposure?\n\n7) Real Discipline Doesn’t Demand Loyalty\n\nVerification is not worship.\n\nIf a “truth cell” asks you to cut off family, isolate from friends, or treat outsiders as enemies—\n\nThat is not verification.\n\nThat is control.\n\n8) Boring Is a Feature\n\nA real archive looks like this:\n\t•\tfiles\n\t•\tdates\n\t•\thashes\n\t•\ttimestamps\n\t•\tredundancy\n\t•\trestraint\n\nNo drama.\n\nNo brand.\n\nTHE POINT\n\nVerification is not a cult.\n\nIt is hygiene.\n\nIn this age, they will try to make hygiene look insane.\n\nDo not be moved by their theater.\n\nDo not be shamed out of your own eyes.\n\nDon’t chase the feed. Build the test.\nAnd keep your truth plain—\nin the kitchen light.\n\n⸻\n\n[Scene extracted → canon/SCENE_29_THE_BAPTIST_SENDS_A_SIGN.md]",
