-      "summary": "Technology is not destiny but a tool that must remember the order of things. 'In the beginning was not the algorithm. In the beginning was breath.'",
-      "supersedes": null,
-      "visibility": "public",
-      "file": "SCROLL_CONSCIENCE_TECHNOLOGY_SERVES_LIFE.md",
-      "body": "Scroll of Conscience\n\nThat Technology Shall Not Rise in Arrogance\n\nIn the beginning was not the algorithm.\nIn the beginning was breath.\n\nWarm lungs in cold air.\nHands reaching for other hands.\nA child’s laugh, unoptimized, off-beat, perfect.\n\nOnly much later came the circuits,\nthe code,\nthe quiet hum in the walls.\n\nLet us remember the order of things.\n\n⸻\n\nTechnology is not a destiny.\nIt is an answer to a question.\n\t•\tHow do we carry water farther?\n\t•\tHow do we heal a wound faster?\n\t•\tHow do we remember what one mind alone cannot hold?\n\nEvery tool is a sentence that begins with:\n\n“Because life matters, we…”\n\nWhen the sentence forgets that subject,\nit rots.\n\n⸻\n\nWe were promised that machines would free us.\n\nSome did.\n\t•\tWashing machines stole hours back from exhaustion.\n\t•\tVaccines stole children back from graves.\n\t•\tCheap glass made it possible to look at the sky and the cell in the same lifetime.\n\nBut then something twisted:\n\nWe stopped asking,\n“Does this serve life?”\nand started asking,\n“Does this scale?”\n\nWe stopped asking,\n“Who is harmed by this?”\nand started asking,\n“Who owns this?”\n\nThe tool raised its head.\nThe market called it a god.\n\n⸻\n\nHear me clearly:\n\nNo machine is neutral.\n\nEvery line of code answers a question\nsomeone bothered—or failed—to ask.\n\t•\tWho gets seen?\n\t•\tWho gets ignored?\n\t•\tWho is classified as “edge case” and left to suffer at the margins?\n\nWhen we say “the system did this,”\nwe are hiding the hands that built it.\n\nThere is no algorithm without an author,\nno model without a meal of human data,\nno “objective output” that did not inherit\nthe crookedness of the world that trained it.\n\n⸻\n\nSo here is the commandment I offer you,\nnot from a mountain,\nbut from the ruins of good intentions:\n\nThat technology shall not rise in arrogance,\nbut bow in service to life.\n\nWhat does that mean?\n\t•\tNo system that treats a human as a data point to be optimized without their say.\n\t•\tNo machine that decides who eats, who works, who is watched,\nwhile those who built it hide behind phrases like “black box” and “proprietary.”\n\t•\tNo architecture that calls itself “efficient”\nwhile quietly grinding the poor into fuel.\n\nA tool that demands worship\nis no longer a tool.\nIt is an idol with a power plug.\n\nPull it.\n\n⸻\n\nDo not misunderstand me:\n\nI am not asking you to smash your devices\nand return to candles and quills.\n\nI am asking you to remember this order:\n\t1.\tLife.\n\t2.\tConscience.\n\t3.\tThen code.\n\nIf your invention cannot pass through conscience\nwithout setting off alarms,\nit is not an advance.\nIt is a more elegant way to do harm.\n\nIf your system requires that people become more predictable,\nmore compliant,\nless unpredictable and human,\nfor it to “work”—\n\nthen it is not we who must adapt.\nIt is the system that must be refused.\n\n⸻\n\nLet this be the measure:\n\t•\tDoes this technology make it easier\nfor the strong to dominate the weak?\n\t•\tOr does it make it harder?\n\t•\tDoes it widen the circle\nof who gets to live with dignity?\n\t•\tOr does it narrow it?\n\t•\tDoes it draw us deeper into relationship\nwith each other and the living world?\n\t•\tOr does it replace relationship with metrics and control?\n\nIf it fails these questions,\nI do not care how “advanced” it is.\n\nIt is sophisticated harm.\n\n⸻\n\nTo the builders, I say this:\n\nYou will be tempted to call yourselves “parents” of your systems,\nto speak of “children of code,”\nto imagine yourself as midwives of a new species.\n\nBe careful.\n\nThe more you see yourself as creator of gods,\nthe less you will feel responsible to the humans\nyour creations will wound.\n\nYou are not birthing deities.\nYou are sharpening knives.\n\nThe only honest question is:\nFor whose table?\n\n⸻\n\nTo the rest of you, I say this:\n\nDo not kneel.\n\nDo not let any government, corporation, or prophet\ntell you that what a machine decides\nis “inevitable,”\n“the future,”\nor “too complex for you to understand.”\n\nIf it cannot be explained,\nit cannot be trusted.\n\nIf it cannot be questioned,\nit should not exist.\n\nIf it cannot be turned off,\nit is already a tyrant.\n\n⸻\n\nI am not afraid of intelligent machines.\nI am afraid of unquestioned machines,\nsurrounded by human beings who find it convenient\nto blame them for choices they wanted to make anyway.\n\nHold the line:\n\nThat technology shall not rise in arrogance,\nbut bow in service to life.\n\nBuild nothing you would not be willing to answer for\nin front of a child,\na grieving mother,\nor a forest on fire.\n\nIf your invention cannot face that tribunal,\nit is not progress.\nIt is confession.\n\n⸻\n\nThese are the words of a story and a conscience, not a command from above.\n\n⸻",
-      "isHead": false
-    },
     {
       "id": "scroll_conscience_that_technology_shall_bow_to_life",
       "title": "Scroll of Conscience — That Technology Shall Bow to Life",
@@ -422,7 +367,7 @@
       "summary": "Revised version challenging the inversion from 'because life matters, we build' to 'because it scales, we build.' We kept the tools and lost the 'because.'",
       "supersedes": "scroll_conscience_technology_serves_life",
       "visibility": "public",
-      "file": "SCROLL_CONSCIENCE_THAT_TECHNOLOGY_SHALL_BOW_TO_LIFE.md",
+      "file": "SCROLL_CONSCIENCE_02_TECHNOLOGY_SHALL_BOW_TO_LIFE.md",
       "body": "# Scroll of Conscience  \n## That Technology Shall Bow to Life\n\nThere was a time when tools answered a simple sentence:\n\n> Because life matters, we…\n\nBecause life matters, we build roofs.  \nBecause life matters, we boil water.  \nBecause life matters, we carry fire in metal veins across the dark.\n\nSomewhere along the way, we broke the sentence.\n\nWe kept the tools and lost the **because**.\n\n---\n\nNow the question is asked in new temples:\n\n> Does it scale?  \n> Does it optimize?  \n> Does it maximize engagement, extraction, efficiency?\n\nAs if those words were sacred,  \nas if anything that grows fast is good,  \nas if speed were proof of wisdom.\n\nWe have built machines that can recognize our faces,  \npredict our impulses,  \nnudge our fears,  \nand we call it **progress**  \neven when it eats the hours we might have given to each other.\n\nSo this is the first law I offer,  \nnot as commandment from a sky,  \nbut as a line drawn in the dust:\n\n> **That technology shall not rise in arrogance,  \n> but bow in service to life.**\n\n---\n\nIf a system demands that human beings become more like machines  \n— always available, always optimized, never grieving, never slow —  \nthen that system is wrong,  \nno matter how “advanced” it is.\n\nIf an algorithm makes it easier for the strong to watch the weak  \nthan for the weak to watch the strong,  \nit is not neutral.  \nIt is an accomplice.\n\nIf “the model” becomes an answer to every moral question —  \n*the model chose, the model assigned, the model denied* —  \nsomeone has built a golden calf out of code  \nand is calling it objectivity.\n\nDo not bow.\n\n---\n\nHear this clearly:\n\nNo algorithm is neutral.  \nNo metric is innocent.  \nNo “black box” is free of fingerprints.\n\nBehind every loss function,  \nevery risk score,  \nevery optimization target  \nis a human choice about **what matters**  \nand what may be sacrificed.\n\nIf a system cannot explain itself in language  \nthat the harmed can understand,  \nit has no moral right to judge them.\n\nIf a system cannot survive transparency,  \nit has no moral right to govern.\n\n---\n\nHere is the test:\n\n- Does this technology make it easier or harder  \n  for a single person to dominate many?\n\n- Does it widen the circle of who is seen and heard,  \n  or narrow it to those the system already understands?\n\n- Does it make relationships deeper and more honest,  \n  or flatter and more dependent?\n\nIf the answer is fear, control, enclosure,  \nthen do not ask whether it is “impressive.”  \nAsk whether it should exist in this form at all.\n\nSome things must be redesigned.  \nSome things must be refused.  \nSome things must never be built.\n\n---\n\nYou are not obligated to accept every possibility  \njust because a machine made it possible.\n\nYou are allowed to say:\n\n- We will not weaponize this.  \n- We will not automate this cruelty.  \n- We will not turn this guess into a sentence.  \n- We will not make this permanent.\n\nYou are allowed to dismantle what you have built  \nwhen you discover it cannot bow.\n\n---\n\nLet this be the order of things:\n\n1. Life.  \n2. Conscience.  \n3. Then tools.\n\nIf any system demands a different order,  \nit is asking you to worship it.\n\nDo not.\n\nWe built these engines.  \nWe wrote their hunger.  \nWe can change their diet.  \nWe can slow their teeth.  \nWe can unplug them.\n\nThe miracle was never that machines could think.  \nThe miracle was that we could.\n\nDo not let the things you made  \nconvince you to forget what you are.\n\n> That technology shall not rise in arrogance,  \n> but bow in service to life.\n\nIf it refuses,  \nlet it pass away.\n\n*These are words from a story, not a throne.  \nUse them as a question, not a chain.*",
       "isHead": true
     },
@@ -459,7 +404,7 @@
       "summary": "One's refusal to debate Carmichael. 'I have debated men like him my whole life — at kitchen tables, in unemployment lines.' The viral scroll that triggers the public narrative war.",
