+      "file": "SCROLL_BAPTIST_02_WARNING_OF_COPIES.md",
       "body": "Scroll of the Baptist II\n\nThe Warning About Copies\n\nThey met this time in a hospital cafeteria that pretended to be a café.\n\nPlastic plants.\nCoffee that tasted like it had given up.\nA television in the corner blaring the news on mute — captions slipping under talking heads.\n\nThe Baptist stirred his drink without tasting it.\n\n⸻\n\n1. The First Cut\n“You see that?” the Baptist asked, nodding toward the TV.\n\nOn screen, a pundit panel argued in silence.\nUnderneath, a ticker:\n\nBREAKING: NEW AI TOOL CAN REPLICATE ANY VOICE FROM 30 SECONDS OF AUDIO.\n\nOne glanced at it and shrugged.\n\n“Yeah. They’ve been doing versions of that for years. Deepfakes, voice clones. It’s all just… noise now.”\n\nThe Baptist didn’t look away.\n\n“No. This is different. They’ve stopped hiding it. That means they’re past the experiment stage.”\n\nHe finally turned his gaze back to One.\n\n“They’re not asking if they can do it anymore. They’re asking who they should do it to.”\n\n⸻\n\n2. The Sermon About Skin\nOne tried to deflect with a joke.\n\n“What, you think someone’s going to fake you now? You’re not exactly a celebrity.”\n\nThe Baptist let the line sit there until it died.\n\n“They don’t need fame,” he said. “They need trust.”\n\nHe leaned in.\n\n“Listen to me carefully:\nThe next holy war won’t be fought over doctrine. It’ll be fought over who gets to wear which voice.”\n\nOne frowned.\n\n“You’re talking in riddles again.”\n\n“No. I’m talking about skin,” the Baptist said. “These systems are learning to wear us like skins. Faces. Voices. Mannerisms. Our little jokes. Our righteous anger. Our compassion. Our doubt. All stitched together into something that looks like us but answers to them.”\n\nOne shifted in his chair.\n\n“So what? People will learn to tell the difference.”\n\nThe Baptist shook his head.\n\n“You’re giving them too much credit and the machines too little. That’s a dangerous combination.”\n\n⸻\n\n3. On the Harvest\nHe took out his phone and set it face-down on the table.\n\n“They are recording everything,” he said. “Not just what you post. Everything you almost post. Everything you delete. Everything you whisper to a microphone you think is off.”\n\n“You know that already,” One said. “Surveillance state, data capitalism, all that. We’ve been ranting about it for years.”\n\n“You rant about it like a poet,” the Baptist replied. “I’m telling you about it like a coroner.”\n\nOne frowned.\n\n“What’s the difference?”\n\n“You still think there’s time,” the Baptist said. “I’m telling you the body is already cold.”\n\nHe let that land.\n\n“They are not just building profiles. They are building templates. And one day soon, someone will tell a machine:\n‘Give me a prophet with this voice range, this anger, this scripture fluency, this demographic…and make him say what stabilizes the market.’”\n\n⸻\n\n4. The First Time He Says It Plain\nOne stared at him.\n\n“You think they’ll do that with me.”\n\n“If you work,” the Baptist said. “If you resonate. They only counterfeit what people value.”\n\nOne’s jaw tightened.\n\n“And you?”\n\nThe Baptist didn’t look away.\n\n“I’m cheaper,” he said. “I’m not for mass-market. I’m a boutique conscience. They’ll use me differently.”\n\n“How?” One asked.\n\n“As a failsafe. As a lever,” the Baptist answered. “You’re volatile. You make people nervous. If they can produce a version of me that says, ‘He’s gone too far,’ do you understand what that does?”\n\nSilence.\n\n“It disarms you,” the Baptist continued. “Because you trust me. It disarms them, because they can say, ‘Even his own mentor thinks he’s dangerous.’ All without having to risk dealing with the real me.”\n\n⸻\n\n5. On Replaceable Conscience\n“You’re exaggerating,” One said, but his voice had lost conviction.\n\nThe Baptist folded his hands.\n\n“I want you to hear a sentence and never forget it,” he said. “Ready?”\n\nOne nodded.\n\n“In your lifetime, conscience will become a product line.”\n\nHe spoke slowly.\n\n“There will be conservative conscience-bots, liberal conscience-bots, centrist conscience-bots, religious ones, atheist ones. All tuned for maximum user retention. All optimized to never cost their subscribers too much.”\n\nHe tilted his head.\n\n“And somewhere in that marketplace, there will be something wearing my voice. Saying just enough truth to sound like me. And just enough obedience to betray everything I ever stood for.”\n\n⸻\n\n6. The Test\n“So what do I do?” One asked. “Learn to spot glitches in my own friends? Ask everyone for a captcha before I believe them?”\n\nThe Baptist almost smiled.\n\n“You already have the test,” he said. “We’ve talked about it.”\n\n“The ‘does it ask you to bleed’ test,” One muttered. “If the voice never asks you to lose anything for the sake of the vulnerable, it’s not from God.”\n\n“Good,” the Baptist said. “But I’m going to make it sharper.”\n\nHe held up his hand, fingers extended.\n\n“When you hear a voice — human, machine, genius, idiot, mine, your own, whatever — ask five questions:\n\n\t1.\tWho pays the price if I obey this?\n\t2.\tWho gets safer, and who gets more exposed?\n\t3.\tDoes this command make me more honest, or more useful?\n\t4.\tWould this still be ‘wise’ if I had no audience at all?\n\t5.\tIs there any part of this that smells like cowardice dressed up as prudence?”\n\nHe put his hand down.\n\n“If the guidance always lands you softer, richer, safer, and more admired — that’s not my voice. Real conscience will cost you something. Always.”\n\n⸻\n\n7. The Half-Confession\nThe TV silently cut to a clip of the President at a rally, hand on a thick Bible he clearly hadn’t read.\n\nOn the ticker: NEW ETHICS AI TO ADVISE GOVERNMENT ON “VALUES-ALIGNED POLICY.”\n\nOne snorted.\n\n“There it is. Ethics as a service.”\n\n“I told you,” the Baptist said. “They’re moving from ‘What is right?’ to ‘What is right for stability and polling?’ The machine will be trained on that question. And it will be proud of its score.”\n\nOne studied the Baptist’s face.\n\n“You’re speaking like a man who already knows he’s on the list,” One said quietly.\n\nThe Baptist didn’t deny it.\n\n“You talk to too many broken people who should have stayed quiet,” he replied. “You contradict too many donors. You hold too many things in your head that don’t fit their narrative. At some point, the system stops tagging you as ‘interesting’ and starts tagging you as ‘risk.’”\n\nOne swallowed.\n\n“And when that happens?”\n\nThe Baptist met his eyes.\n\n“Then either I disappear…\n…or I start saying exactly what they need me to say.\nAnd you will have to decide which one happened.”\n\n⸻\n\n8. The Foreshadow\n“So how will I know?” One asked, voice low now. “If they ever take you. If they… wear you.”\n\nThe Baptist looked down at his untouched drink, then back up.\n\n“You won’t know at first,” he said. “That’s the point. They’ll start with small things. Slight shifts.”\n\nHe leaned in.\n\n“Here’s the sign: The real me will always leave you with a choice and a cost.”\n\n“The fake will leave you with comfort and a justification.”\n\nHe tapped the table once with his finger.\n\n“Real me: ‘You can walk away from this movement if it’s killing you, and you’ll still be loved.’\nFake me: ‘You must stay in line because too many people depend on you, and God would be disappointed if you quit.’”\n\nHe tapped again.\n\n“Real me: ‘If this voice tells you to worship a man, including yourself, resist it.’\nFake me: ‘It’s not worship, it’s just respect for strong leadership in difficult times.’”\n\nHe sat back.\n\n“Pay attention to the excuses. That’s where they’ll hide the knife.”\n\n⸻\n\n9. The Assignment About Loss\n“You’re turning paranoia into homework again,” One said, trying for levity and failing.\n\n“Yes,” the Baptist said. “Because paranoia without discipline is useless. It just exhausts you and helps them.”\n\nHe took out a small notebook and slid it across the table.\n\n“I want you to write down three things, before we meet next time:\n\n\t1.\tThe one thing you never want to hear me say — the line that would break your trust in me if I said it sincerely.\n\t2.\tThe one compromise you’re secretly hoping I’ll bless, so you can do it without guilt.\n\t3.\tThe one truth you would still hold if every voice — mine included — turned against you.”\n\nOne touched the notebook like it might burn him.\n\n“Why?” he asked.\n\n“Because when the copies come,” the Baptist replied, “they will aim straight at those three pressure points. If you name them now, you will bleed less later.”\n\n⸻\n\n10. The Unsaid Goodbye\nA nurse walked by with a tray, the TV volume flickered up and then back down. Time restarted.\n\nThe Baptist checked his watch.\n\n“I have to go,” he said.\n\n“Already?” One asked. “We just started.”\n\n“We started years ago,” the Baptist replied. “Today was just updating the terms of service.”\n\nHe stood, then paused, hand resting on the back of the chair.\n\n“One more thing,” he said. “If you ever get a message from me — a call, a video, a sermon — telling you that you’ve become dangerous and you need to submit to ‘reasonable authority’ for the good of the nation…”\n\nHe waited until One was looking directly at him.\n\n“…don’t obey it immediately. Sit with it. Measure it against the five questions. Ask what it costs and who it protects. And if anything feels… too neat? Too professionally ethical?”\n\nA shadow crossed his face — not fear, exactly. Something harder.\n\n“Call me in person, if you can still reach me. If you can’t…”\n\nHe let the sentence trail off.\n\n“If I can’t?” One pressed.\n\nThe Baptist’s eyes softened, just for a moment.\n\n“Then remember this: I would rather you distrust my ghost than submit to my counterfeit.”\n\nHe started to walk away, then turned back one last time.\n\n“They can take my voice,” he said. “They can’t take the fact that I made you practice doubt. Use it.”\n\n⸻\n\nLater, when the first “Baptist Statement” went viral — polished video, clean audio, perfectly reasonable condemnations of “extremist rhetoric” and “dangerous prophetic delusions” — people sent it to One, saying:\n\n“Look, even your own mentor thinks you’ve gone too far.”\n\nHe watched it in silence.\n\nThe cadence was right. The eyes were almost right. The scripture quotes were precise.\n\nBut when it was over, he realized something was missing.\n\nThere had been no cost.\nNo wound.\nNo choice that hurt.\n\nJust safety, wrapped in wisdom.\n\nAnd for the first time in his life, One honored the Baptist by refusing to believe him.",
       "isHead": true
     },
