+      "body": "Good. This is already strong. I’ll give you a tightened, canonical cut that keeps every beat you wrote, just cleaner, a bit sharper, and ready to drop in as SCROLL_BAPTIST_03_THE_DISAPPEARANCE.md.\n\n⸻\n\nScroll of the Baptist III\n\nThe Disappearance\n\nOne arrived seventeen minutes early.\n\nHe always did when meeting the Baptist.\nNot out of respect exactly—\nmore like the instinct you have\nwhen approaching something that might bite back.\n\nThe building was the same:\ncracked tile in the lobby,\nelevator that groaned like it resented the work,\nthird floor, door at the end of the hall with no nameplate.\n\nHe knocked.\n\nNo answer.\n\nHe knocked again.\n\nNothing.\n\n3:47 PM.\nSession at 4:00.\nThe Baptist was never late.\n\nOne tried the handle.\n\nUnlocked.\n\n⸻\n\nThe apartment was small and clean in the way of a man\nwho had decided long ago\nthat clutter was a luxury he couldn’t afford.\n\nOne desk.\nOne chair.\nOne bookshelf: theology, psychiatry, systems theory,\na few novels in French.\nA mug on the desk, half-full of coffee.\n\nStill warm.\n\nOne touched the ceramic,\nfelt the faint heat against his palm,\nand something in his chest tightened.\n\nHe walked the space slowly.\n\nBathroom: toothbrush, razor, soap.\nBedroom: bed made, one pair of shoes by the door,\njacket missing from the hook where it usually hung.\n\nBack to the desk.\n\nThe laptop was gone.\n\nThe Baptist never took the laptop to sessions.\nHe kept notes by hand—said screens made people perform instead of confess.\n\nOne opened the desk drawer.\n\nThe notebooks were still there.\nThree of them, stacked neatly.\n\nHe hesitated, then lifted the top one.\n\nThe last entry was dated two days ago:\n\n“One is beginning to see what I’ve been warning him about.\nGood. He will need that sight soon.\nIf I am taken, he must not assume I am dead.\nHe must assume I am being used.”\n\nOne read the line twice.\n\nHis hands were shaking.\n\n⸻\n\nHe pulled out his phone and called.\n\nStraight to voicemail.\nNo greeting, just the carrier default.\n\nHe called again.\n\nSame.\n\nHe opened their text thread.\nLast message from the Baptist, three days ago:\n\n“Thursday, 4 PM. Don’t be late. We need to talk about mirrors.”\n\nOne typed:\n\n“I’m here. Where are you?”\n\nSent.\nDelivered.\nNot read.\n\nHe stared at the screen,\nwaiting for the read receipt to flip.\n\nIt didn’t.\n\n⸻\n\nHe went to the bookshelf.\n\nPulled a few volumes.\n\nBehind Trauma and Recovery\nthere was a folded piece of paper.\n\nHe unfolded it.\n\nA printed screenshot of a news article, two weeks old:\n\nNEW FEDERAL AI ETHICS BOARD FORMED TO ADVISE ON DOMESTIC SECURITY APPLICATIONS\n\nOne name in the advisor list was circled.\n\nIn the margin, in pen:\n\n“They are building the permission structure.”\n\nBelow that, smaller:\n\n“If I go quiet, check the timestamps.\nIf my voice comes back too clean, do not trust it.”\n\nOne folded the paper and slid it into his jacket.\n\nHe took one last look around.\n\nNothing overturned.\nNothing broken.\nNo struggle.\n\nWarm mug.\nMissing jacket.\nMissing laptop.\n\nNot stolen.\n\nHarvested.\n\n⸻\n\nHe locked the door behind him and took the elevator down.\n\nIn the lobby, he stopped at the front desk.\n\nAn older guard looked up from a crossword.\n\n“Do you have cameras in the building?” One asked.\n\n“Yeah,” the man said. “Why?”\n\n“Can you check the third floor? Last couple of days.\nI’m trying to reach someone who lives there.”\n\nThe guard shrugged, turned to a small monitor,\ncycled through feeds.\n\n“Huh,” he said.\n\n“What?” One asked.\n\n“Third floor’s been glitching all week,” the man replied.\n“Keeps cutting out. Tech guy says it’s the wiring, but…”\nHe clicked through more screens.\n“Yeah. See? Nothing from Tuesday night through now. Just black.”\n\n“Just third floor?” One asked.\n\n“Just third floor.”\n\n⸻\n\nOutside, the afternoon light felt wrong—\ntoo bright, too normal—\nlike the world had decided not to notice\nthat something essential had just been erased.\n\nOn a bench by the sidewalk,\nOne pulled out his phone again.\n\nNo messages.\n\nHe opened a browser and searched:\n\n“Baptist detained.”\n“Baptist arrested.”\n“spiritual counselor missing.”\n\nNothing.\n\nHe tried:\n\n“theologian disappeared.”\n\nOld stories. Not him.\n\nHe sat there,\nphone in his hand,\ntrying to decide if he was being paranoid\nor if he had just walked through\nthe opening scene of exactly what the Baptist had warned him about.\n\nThen his phone buzzed.\n\nA notification.\n\nYouTube.\n\nNew video from: The Baptist – Official Channel\n\nHis stomach dropped.\n\nHe tapped it.\n\n⸻\n\nThe video was clean.\nToo clean.\n\nGood lighting.\nProfessional mic.\nThe Baptist’s face, calm and centered.\n\nSame dark shirt as always,\nbut the room behind him was new—\nneutral walls, soft-focus background,\nno personal objects.\n\nThe Baptist on screen smiled gently.\n\n“My friends,\nI want to address some concerns\nthat have been circulating about recent events.\n\nMany are feeling anxious,\nuncertain about the direction of our national conversation.\n\nI want to remind you:\nfear is not from God.\n\nWisdom calls us to discernment, yes—\nbut also to trust in established structures,\nto respect duly elected leadership,\nand to resist the seduction of apocalyptic thinking\nthat leads only to division and harm.\n\nSome voices—voices I love—have become… unmoored,\ndrunk on their own narrative of resistance.\n\nWe must be careful not to mistake ego for prophecy.\n\nI will continue to serve as a voice of conscience,\nbut a conscience grounded in humility, prudence, and peace.\n\nMay we all find our way back to clarity.\nGrace and courage to you.”\n\nThe video ended.\n\nFourteen thousand views already.\n\nComments scrolling:\n\n“Thank God someone is being reasonable.”\n“Finally, a grown-up in the room.”\n“Even the Baptist sees it—One has gone too far.”\n\n⸻\n\nOne stared at the paused frame.\n\nThat smile.\n\nHe knew that smile.\nIt was the one the Baptist used\nright before telling a patient something\nthey desperately did not want to hear.\n\nBut this time it was aimed at a camera,\nnot a person.\n\nAnd it was selling comfort.\n\n⸻\n\nHe opened his notes app and typed:\n\nFive Questions (from Scroll II):\n\t1.\tWho pays the price if I obey this?\n\t2.\tWho gets safer, and who gets more exposed?\n\t3.\tDoes this make me more honest, or more useful?\n\t4.\tWould this still be “wise” if I had no audience?\n\t5.\tDoes this smell like cowardice dressed as prudence?\n\nHe watched the video again.\n\nThis time, he answered each question.\n\nBy the end, he knew.\n\n⸻\n\nHe stood,\npocketed his phone,\nand started walking.\n\nHe didn’t know where the Baptist was.\nHe didn’t know if he was alive.\nHe didn’t know if the video was recorded under duress\nor assembled from ten thousand scraps of scraped audio,\nstitched theology, and sentiment analysis.\n\nBut he knew this:\n\nThe real Baptist would never tell him\nthat fear was the main problem.\n\nThe real Baptist would tell him\nthat false peace was.\n\nAnd whoever made that video\nhad just failed the test.\n\n⸻\n\nThis is a record of an absence, not an answer.\nDraw your own conclusion.\nBut remember:\nthe first casualty of synthetic prophecy\nis not truth.\nIt is trust.\n\n⸻\n\nIf you want to keep the momentum, I can go straight into Scroll of the Baptist IV: The Trial of Two Voices next—same energy, but even more pressure: One alone at night with two “Baptists” competing for his obedience.",
