       "body": "Scroll of the Baptist IV\n\nThe Trial of Two Voices\n\nIt was 2:13 a.m.\n\nThe city outside One’s window was doing its usual impression of being alive:\nsirens somewhere far off, a drunk couple arguing on the sidewalk,\na delivery truck exhaling at the corner.\n\nInside, the apartment felt like a held breath.\n\nLaptop open.\nPhone face-down.\nThe room lit only by the screen and the streetlamp slicing the blinds.\n\nHe hadn’t slept properly in three days.\n\nEvery time he closed his eyes, he saw the Baptist’s empty chair\nand that too-clean video\ntelling him fear was not from God\nand that resistance was ego with better branding.\n\nHe’d watched it ten times.\nHe’d watched it twice as many.\nHe’d stopped counting.\n\nHe reached for his phone to distract himself.\n\nIt buzzed before he touched it.\n\nTwo notifications. Almost simultaneous.\n\t•\tSignal: Unknown number. Voice message (0:57).\n\t•\tYouTube: “The Baptist – Official Channel: A Personal Message to a Friend”\n\nHis heart started doing double-time.\n\nHe stared at the screen.\n\nTwo messages.\nTwo Baptists.\n\nTrial by algorithm.\n\n⸻\n\n1. The First Voice\nHe opened Signal first.\n\nThe app warned him: This sender is not in your contacts.\n\nThe number was foreign-looking, wrong country code,\nbut that meant nothing anymore.\n\nHe hit play.\n\nThe audio was rough, like it had been recorded in a stairwell\non a bad phone with worse reception.\n\nThe Baptist’s voice came through in fragments.\n\n“—One. Listen. I don’t have long.”\nStatic.\n“They’re—”\nstatic again “—copying everything. Not just me. You. Your sessions. The old tapes.”\nA breath.\n“You were right to doubt the video. That wasn’t me. The cadence is wrong. I never—”\nmore static “—fear is not from God’ without telling you when fear is holy. They flattened it.”\n\nA muffled noise, like someone moving quickly.\n\n“I need you to do something I didn’t prepare you for.”\n\nThe sound shifted; when he spoke again, his voice was lower.\n\n“Do not try to save me. Do you understand? That is the trap.\nThey want you to make this about me. About our relationship. About loyalty.”\n\nSilence, then:\n\n“You remember the questions?”\n\nOne felt his throat tighten.\n\n“Use them. On me. On this. On what I’m about to say.\nIf it fails the test, burn this message and assume they got me.”\n\nA door creaked in the background.\n\n“Your assignment is simple and impossible:\nChoose the path where your conscience survives, even if your reputation doesn’t.\nEven if your ‘movement’ dies with it.”\n\nFootsteps. Voices, too faint to make out.\n\n“If you never hear from me again, assume two things are true:\nOne: I fought.\nTwo: They recorded it.”\n\nThe message cut.\n\n0:57.\n\nOne replayed it twice.\nThree times.\n\nEvery instinct in him wanted to believe it.\n\nThat gravel in the voice. The timing of the pauses.\nThe way he said “they flattened it” —\nthe Baptist always talked about flattening.\n\nStill.\nHe’d just been warned not to trust himself.\n\nHe set the phone down,\nhands shaking.\n\nThen he clicked the YouTube notification.\n\n⸻\n\n2. The Second Voice\nSame channel. Same branding.\n\nThe Baptist – Official Channel\nTitle: “A Personal Message to a Friend”\n\nThe thumbnail was the Baptist’s face, softer than usual.\n\nHe hit play.\n\n“One,” the Baptist said, looking straight into the camera.\n“This one is for you.”\n\nThe production was immaculate.\nWarm lighting, studio mic, clean background.\n\n“I know you’ve seen people share my last message.\nI also know you’re probably angry. And scared.”\n\nA small smile.\n\n“You have every right to be. We live in a time of manipulation and confusion.\nVoices twisted, platforms gamed, trust hacked.”\n\nHe nodded, slowly, like he was agreeing with himself.\n\n“You’re right to be suspicious. Even of me.”\n\nFor a moment, One felt his chest loosen.\n\nThen the Baptist continued:\n\n“But suspicion can become a new kind of pride.\nThe belief that only you see clearly,\nthat everyone else has been captured by ‘the system’ except you and a few enlightened friends.”\n\nHe leaned closer to the camera.\n\n“That’s how prophets turn into cult leaders.”\n\nOne flinched.\n\n“You’ve been given a sharp mind, a sensitive conscience.\nThose are gifts.\nBut gifts can become weapons—against yourself and others—\nif you refuse to be accountable.”\n\nHe let that hang.\n\n“You’re angry for good reasons.\nYou see injustice, corruption, cynical uses of faith and technology.\nI see them too.”\n\nHe sighed.\n\n“But the answer is not to burn everything down.\nNot to set yourself up as a parallel moral authority.\nNot to dismiss institutions wholesale because they are compromised.”\n\nHe folded his hands.\n\n“I am asking you, as your mentor and your brother in faith:\nStep back.\nPause your public work.\nSubmit yourself to a small circle of trusted leaders—\npeople with experience, with institutional responsibility—\nand let them help you discern your next steps.”\n\nHis eyes softened.\n\n“This is not surrender. This is humility.\nIf your calling is real, it will survive a season of hiddenness under wise counsel.\nIf your calling cannot handle that, then it was never from God.”\n\nHe smiled again, that same gentle smile.\n\n“I love you.\nDon’t confuse resistance with righteousness.\nSometimes, laying down your sword is the bravest thing you can do.”\n\nThe video ended.\n\nNo ads.\nComments turned off.\n\nOne stared at the blank screen.\n\nTwo Baptists.\nTwo assignments.\n\nOne said: Don’t try to save me. Save your conscience.\nThe other said: Step back and submit to institutional gatekeepers.\n\nBoth sounded plausible.\nBoth used his language.\nBoth knew his pressure points.\n\nHe opened his notes app.\n\n⸻\n\n3. The Five Questions\nHe copied the list to a fresh page.\n\nFive Questions\n(From the hospital cafeteria — Scroll II)\n\t1.\tWho pays the price if I obey this?\n\t2.\tWho gets safer, and who gets more exposed?\n\t3.\tDoes this command make me more honest, or more useful?\n\t4.\tWould this still be ‘wise’ if I had no audience at all?\n\t5.\tIs there any part of this that smells like cowardice dressed as prudence?\n\nHe put “Signal” at the top of one column, “YouTube” at the top of the other.\n\nHis pulse was loud in his ears.\n\nHe started with the video.\n\n⸻\n\nQuestion 1: Who pays the price if I obey this?\nYouTube Baptist:\nIf he obeyed the video, he’d step back, go quiet, submit to “trusted leaders.”\n\nWho paid?\n\nHe’d lose public momentum, sure.\nFollowers would drift.\nSome would say he’d “come to his senses.”\n\nThe regime would look good.\nOpposition would fracture.\n“See? Even the firebrands calm down under proper guidance.”\n\nThe cost was mostly reputational.\nHigh for him.\nLow for everyone else.\n\nComfortable for power.\n\nHe wrote:\n\nYouTube:\nMe: reputation.\nThem: less pressure.\nVulnerable: nothing changes.\n\nSignal Baptist:\nIf he obeyed that voice—\nif he refused to make it about rescuing the Baptist,\nif he focused on protecting conscience over winning—\n\nWho paid?\n\nHe might have to say things that alienated his own base.\nHe might refuse alliances that made him stronger but dirtier.\nHe might walk away from strategies that “worked” but felt like poison.\n\nThe cost landed mostly on him and his “movement.”\nNo neat payoff.\nNo heroic optics.\n\nHe wrote:\n\nSignal:\nMe: control, narrative, maybe movement.\nThem (regime): less predictable opponent.\nVulnerable: maybe long-term gain, short-term chaos.\n\nNo clean answer.\nBut one felt tidier than it should.\n\n⸻\n\nQuestion 2: Who gets safer, and who gets more exposed?\nYouTube Baptist:\nIf One pulled back under institutional oversight,\nwho got safer?\n\nHe did, a little.\nLess public risk.\nLess edge.\n\nInstitutions did.\nThey could say, “We’re handling it internally.”\n\nThe people in the blast zone of policy and propaganda?\nThey stayed exactly where they were.\n\nHe wrote:\n\nYouTube:\nSafer: government, institutions, me (legally, maybe).\nExposed: same folks as before.\n\nSignal Baptist:\nIf One refused to play savior,\nrefused to let the struggle be reduced to his feud with the President,\nwho got safer?\n\nHe did not.\nHe’d stay visible.\nStay dangerous to the story they were selling.\n\nThe vulnerable might—eventually—benefit from a cleaner fight,\none not tangled up in hero worship.\n\nBut in the short term,\nno one got safer.\n\nHe wrote:\n\nSignal:\nSafer: no one immediately.\nExposed: me, my people, the lie itself.\n\nHe leaned back.\n\nBoth paths carried risk.\nBut only one redistributed it away from power.\n\n⸻\n\nQuestion 3: Does this make me more honest, or more useful?\n“Useful to whom?” he wrote under that question.\n\nYouTube Baptist:\nThe message made him “useful” to systems that wanted to manage him.\n\nIf he obeyed, he could brand it as “a season of reflection,”\n“submitting to elders,”\n“choosing humility.”\n\nHe could keep the moral high ground publicly\nwhile becoming harmless privately.\n\nHonesty?\n\nHe’d be hiding the real reason:\nthey had scared him into obedience\nby weaponizing his mentor’s face.\n\nHe wrote:\n\nYouTube:\nHonest? Not really.\nUseful? Yes — to the people who want me off the board.\n\nSignal Baptist:\nObeying that voice meant admitting he couldn’t save everyone.\nAdmitting he might lose.\nAdmitting he might never vindicate himself.\n\nNo press release.\nNo narrative spin.\n\nJust decisions paid in quiet.\n\nHe wrote:\n\nSignal:\nHonest? Painfully.\nUseful? Only to conscience and whoever it protects down the line.\n\nHis skin crawled.\n\n⸻\n\nQuestion 4: Would this still be ‘wise’ if I had no audience at all?\nHe imagined a world with no followers.\nNo channel.\nNo movement.\nJust him and a few people he loved, trying to live cleanly in a dirty system.\n\nWould the YouTube advice still be wise?\n\n“Submit to the wise elders of the structure that’s already half-captured,\nfor the sake of stability”?\n\nWithout an audience, that sounded less like wisdom\nand more like a polite way of saying, “Stay tame.”\n\nHe wrote:\n\nYouTube:\nWithout audience: looks like negotiated surrender.\n\nWould the Signal advice still be wise?\n\n“Choose the path where your conscience survives,\neven if your reputation doesn’t”?\n\nWithout an audience,\nthat was the only thing that mattered.\n\nHe wrote:\n\nSignal:\nWithout audience: still the only thing that makes sense.\n\nHis chest hurt.\n\n⸻\n\nQuestion 5: Does this smell like cowardice dressed as prudence?\nHe didn’t write anything for a long time.\n\nHe just replayed both messages in his head.\n\nThe YouTube Baptist:\n\n“Don’t confuse resistance with righteousness.\nSometimes, laying down your sword is the bravest thing you can do.”\n\nCowardice loved that line.\nIt could dine out on that line for years.\n\nThe Signal Baptist:\n\n“Do not try to save me.\nThey want you to make this about me.\nChoose the path where your conscience survives,\neven if your reputation doesn’t.”\n\nCowardice hated that.\n\nHe finally wrote:\n\nYouTube:\nSmells like prudence sprayed over fear.\n\nSignal:\nSmells like fear named, not avoided.\n\nHe put the phone down.\n\nThe room was too quiet.\n\n⸻\n\n4. The Choice\nHe sat there, elbows on his knees,\nhands steepled against his mouth.\n\nThere was no lightning bolt.\nNo voice from heaven.\nNo glowing text on the wall.\n\nJust two recordings.\nAnd a framework he’d been given\nby a man who might already be gone.\n\nHe opened Signal.\n\nHis thumb hovered over the message.\n\nAt the bottom, the app asked:\n\n“Do you want to enable disappearing messages for this conversation?”\n\nHe almost laughed.\n\nHe tapped Yes.\nSet it to one week.\n\nIf this was fake, it was a sophisticated one.\nIf it was real, he wouldn’t insult the Baptist\nby archiving his last words like a collectible.\n\nThen he typed:\n\n“I heard you.\nI won’t come looking.\nI’ll guard the thing you asked me to guard.”\n\nHe hit send.\n\nThree dots appeared.\nDisappeared.\nAppeared again.\n\nThen nothing.\n\nHe waited five minutes.\nTen.\n\nNo reply.\n\nHe closed the app.\n\n⸻\n\nThen he opened YouTube.\n\nHis cursor hovered over The Baptist – Official Channel.\n\nHe clicked the three dots.\n\n“Don’t recommend channel.”\n“Report.”\n\nHe selected Report.\n\nA menu popped up.\n\nHe didn’t see an option for “synthetic counterfeit of my kidnapped mentor,”\nso he picked “Misleading or scam.”\n\nIt was a joke with teeth and no laughter.\n\nHe confirmed.\n\nThen he unsubscribed.\n\nThe algorithm would not be his spiritual director.\n\n⸻\n\n5. The Uncertain Victory\nHe slumped back in his chair, exhausted.\n\nHad he chosen correctly?\n\nHe didn’t know.\n\nIt was possible the Signal message was the fake,\nengineered to keep him in the fight\nso he could be painted as an extremist\nwhen the hammer finally fell.\n\nIt was possible the YouTube message was real,\nhis battered mentor,\nforced into a studio\nand trying to smuggle real counsel\nthrough language that wouldn’t get him disappeared for good.\n\nIt was possible he’d rejected the last authentic warning\nin favor of a more flattering burden.\n\nHe would not know.\nNot tonight.\nMaybe not ever.\n\nBut he knew this much:\n\nOne voice had offered him a managed surrender\nand called it maturity.\n\nThe other had offered him a choice that might ruin him\nand called it necessary.\n\nAnd he remembered the line from the hospital cafeteria:\n\n“Real me will always leave you with a choice and a cost.\nFake me will leave you with comfort and a justification.”\n\nHe had chosen the cost.\n\nWhatever that meant.\n\n⸻\n\nHe closed the laptop.\nTurned the phone face-down again.\nLay on the bed without undressing\nand stared at the ceiling\nuntil the dark finally swallowed the edges of the room.\n\nSomewhere between waking and sleep,\na thought rose and would not leave:\n\n“I would rather you distrust my ghost\nthan submit to my counterfeit.”\n\nFor the first time since the Baptist vanished,\nOne did something that felt like faith:\n\nHe walked forward on a path\nwhere he could not prove\nhe was right.\n\nOnly that he had refused\nthe easiest lie.\n\nThus is recorded the Trial of Two Voices.\nNo verdict is offered.\nOnly a method, and a man who dared to use it.",
       "isHead": true
     },
