       "body": "Scroll of Sandi III\n\nThe Child of Two Kingdoms\n\nThe test strip turned positive in under a minute.\n\nFor a woman who lived on polling margins and approval curves,\nthere was something obscene about an answer that clear.\n\nNo recount.\nNo spin.\nNo recounting the same event three ways in three news cycles.\n\nJust two lines.\n\nShe sat on the closed toilet lid, the bathroom fan humming like bad white noise,\nand watched her hands tremble around a piece of plastic\nthat had just redrawn the map of the future.\n\n⸻\n\nHer first thought was not mother.\n\nHer first thought was leverage.\n\nShe hated that.\n\nThe second thought came fast behind it:\n\nMy father will see this as succession.\nOne will see it as prophecy.\nNo one will see it as a person but me.\n\nShe leaned forward, elbows on her knees,\nforehead pressed against her fists,\nand let herself breathe in four-counts like the therapist had taught her\nafter the second assassination attempt on her father:\n\nIn. Two. Three. Four.\nHold. Two. Three. Four.\nOut. Two. Three. Four.\n\nThe therapist had said it was for anxiety.\nSandi knew it was also for secrecy.\nBreathing exercises were the only time in her day\nno one expected words.\n\n⸻\n\nLater, when she stepped back into the small apartment living room,\nOne was at the table, hunched over a notebook,\nwriting like the pen was the only thing keeping the ceiling up.\n\nHe looked up when he heard her.\n\nHe always did.\nLike his nervous system was tuned to her footsteps.\n\n“You okay?” he asked.\n\nNo preamble.\nHe read faces faster than headline feeds.\n\n“I’m late,” she said.\n\nHis brow furrowed.\n\n“For what? We’re not—”\n\n“Not for the meeting,” she cut him off. “For my period.”\n\nIt took him a second.\n\nHe blinked.\n\n“Ah,” he said.\n\nIt wasn’t the response she expected.\n\nNot joy. Not panic.\n\nJust ah—like a man who’d known this was statistically likely and spiritually loaded.\n\n“How late?” he asked.\n\n“Two weeks,” she said.\n\n“You tested?”\n\nShe nodded.\n\nHe stood up slowly, like gravity had doubled.\n\n“Okay,” he said. “Okay.”\n\nShe waited.\n\nThis was the moment she’d been dreading:\nthe prophetic monologue,\nthe “this is the child the scrolls spoke of,”\nthe weight of two thousand years of religious neurosis\ndumped on a cluster of dividing cells.\n\nInstead, he crossed the room,\nstopped a foot in front of her,\nand asked the one question nobody in her life had ever started with:\n\n“Do you want this?”\n\nIt almost knocked her over.\n\nShe opened her mouth to say the responsible thing:\n\nNow is not the time.\nThis is dangerous.\nWe can’t.\n\nWhat came out instead was:\n\n“I don’t know.”\n\nHonesty, unplanned.\n\nHe nodded slowly.\n\n“Okay,” he said again. “That’s allowed.”\n\n⸻\n\nThey sat on the couch.\n\nShe explained it like she would explain a risk forecast:\n\t•\tPregnancy meant vulnerability.\n\t•\tVulnerability meant leverage.\n\t•\tLeverage meant her father would move fast.\n\n“He will not let this be neutral,” she said.\n“He will brand this child before it has a name.”\n\n“How?” One asked.\n\n“Patriotic miracle. Dynasty. ‘Proof that God blesses our line.’\nDoesn’t matter the exact wording. He’ll commission a slogan.”\n\n“And you?” One asked. “What’s your slogan?”\n\nShe stared at the coffee table.\n\n“Too late to back out, too early to celebrate,” she said. “That’s where my head is.”\n\nHe almost smiled.\n\n“That’s honest,” he said.\n\nShe exhaled sharply.\n\n“You know what scares me?” she continued. “You.”\n\n“Fair,” he said.\n\n“You’re already telling yourself stories,” she said.\n“I can see it. Your ‘child of two worlds,’ ‘bridge-baby,’ whatever working title your messiah complex has cooked up.”\n\nHe didn’t deny it.\n\n“Of course I’ve thought it,” he said.\n“How could I not? The daughter of Carmichael and the man they’re already calling his opposite have a child? Every symbol junkie in heaven and hell is taking notes.”\n\nHe met her eyes.\n\n“But listen to me: I will not turn this kid into a flag.”\n\n“Will not, or don’t want to?” she asked.\n\nHe winced. Fair hit.\n\n“Don’t want to,” he said. “So you’ll have to help make sure I don’t.”\n\n⸻\n\nShe leaned back, staring at the ceiling.\n\n“You once said,” she murmured, “that you thought a child like this could eventually help unite the country. Maybe the world.”\n\n“I did,” he said.\n\n“And do you still believe that?” she asked.\n\nHe thought for a long moment.\n\n“I believe the possibility exists,” he said.\n“But not as destiny. As risk.”\n\n“Risk?” she repeated.\n\n“If we’re not careful,” he said, “this child becomes a symbol people kill and legislate around.\nThe ‘heir of the Prophet.’\nThe ‘redeemed bloodline of Carmichael.’\nA walking merger—too valuable to be human.”\n\nHe rubbed his face with both hands.\n\n“I don’t want that,” he said. “God knows I don’t want that.\nBut you can feel it, right? The pull? The narrative gravity?”\n\nShe did.\nIt scared her more than the pregnancy.\n\n⸻\n\n“Tell me the worst-case scenario,” she said.\n“Out loud. Use that sick prophet brain.”\n\nHe didn’t hesitate.\n\n“Worst case?” he said.\n“The regime captures the story. Your father spins it as sign of divine favor.\nMy people spin it as a messianic omen.\nThe kid grows up inside a crossfire of expectations so heavy\nthey either break, disappear, or turn into exactly the kind of tyrant I’ve been screaming about.”\n\nHe met her eyes.\n\n“Worst case, we become the origin story of the next Antichrist.”\n\nShe swallowed.\n\n“Okay,” she said. “And the best case?”\n\n“Best case,” he said slowly, “is that we treat this child like a person, not a prophecy.\nThat we protect their right to be boring, wrong, ordinary.\nThat we build systems and alliances strong enough\nthat no one ever needs them to be a savior.”\n\nHe exhaled.\n\n“And if, in thirty or forty years, they decide to step into the mess,\nit’s because they chose it with full information,\nnot because we dropped them on an altar before they could walk.”\n\n⸻\n\nSandi laughed once, harsh and small.\n\n“You realize how insane it is,” she said,\n“that ‘they get to be ordinary’ counts as a radical best-case scenario?”\n\n“Welcome to our empire,” he said.\n\nSilence settled over them.\n\nShe put a hand over her stomach.\n\n“So,” she said quietly, “if we keep this child… what do we tell them, when they’re old enough to understand?”\n\nHe thought about it.\n\n“Not that they were born to save the world,” he said.\n“That’s the lie my Church told me.”\n\nHe looked at her.\n\n“I think we tell them: You were born into a war over what makes a human life worth living. We chose you over that war. The rest is negotiation.”\n\nSomething in her chest unclenched.\n\n⸻\n\nShe stood.\n\n“Okay,” she said. “Then here’s my condition.”\n\nHe looked up.\n\n“You don’t use our child in your speeches,” she said.\n“Not as metaphor. Not as prophecy. Not as proof.”\n\n“I—”\n\n“I’m not finished,” she said.\n\nHe shut his mouth.\n\n“You don’t float them as a campaign symbol.\nYou don’t allow anyone to call them ‘the bridge,’ ‘the heir,’ or any other bullshit title.\nAnd if your people start doing it behind your back,\nyou shut it down publicly or I walk.”\n\n“Walk where?” he asked.\n\nShe shrugged.\n\n“Anywhere that’s not your shadow,” she said.\n“I’ve already grown up in one man’s myth. I’m not raising a child in another.”\n\nHe swallowed.\n\n“Done,” he said.\n\n“Say it properly,” she insisted.\n\nHe nodded.\n\n“I will not turn our child into a symbol,” he said.\n“I will not let my followers turn them into a banner.\nIf they try, I will correct them in public, at cost to myself.”\n\nShe watched his face for signs of spin.\n\nFound none.\n\n“For my part,” she said, “I will not let my father turn this child into a dynastic asset.\nIf he tries, I’ll burn whatever I have to burn to stop it.”\n\n“That might start a war,” One said.\n\nShe nodded.\n\n“Then the war starts,” she said.\n“But it won’t start inside their crib.”\n\n⸻\n\nLater, when she lay awake and he finally slept,\nshe stared at the ceiling and tried to imagine a future\nwhere their child wasn’t a headline,\na prophecy fulfillment,\na battlefield.\n\nAll she could manage was an image:\n\nA small hand, sticky with juice,\ntugging at her sleeve,\nnot to ask about destiny\nbut to say,\n\n“Come see. There’s a bird outside.”\n\nShe held onto that.\n\nNot world unity.\nNot global reconciliation.\n\nJust the possibility that someone born of that much chaos\nmight one day point at something simple and alive\nand know, for a few seconds,\nthat they were allowed to enjoy it.\n\n⸻\n\nThis is the record of the moment\nSandi Carmichael realized that carrying a child\nmeant carrying a line in the sand\nno strategist, president, prophet, or algorithm\nwould be allowed to cross.\n\nWhether the world would honor that line\nwas another question.\n\nBut for the first time in a long time,\nshe knew exactly what side of it she was on.",
       "isHead": true
     },
