       "body": "Scroll of Carmichael II\n\nThe Bullet That Missed\n\nThey told him later it was a miracle.\n\nHe didn’t need them to.\n\nHe’d already decided that himself.\n\n⸻\n\nThe rally was routine.\nNothing he hadn’t done a hundred times.\n\nFlag choreography.\nPre-vetted “ordinary citizens” behind him on stage.\nCrowd thick with merch and heatstroke.\n\nHe was mid-sentence when the air changed.\n\nHe didn’t hear the first shot.\nHe felt the pause.\n\nThe odd, animal silence that falls on a crowd\nhalf a heartbeat before it understands it’s in danger.\n\nThen the sound hit—a crack that didn’t belong to fireworks or speakers.\n\nThe Secret Service moved as one organism:\nhand on his back, hand on his shoulder,\ndragging him down behind the podium.\n\nSomeone screamed.\nSomeone else yelled, “Get down!” as if anyone needed the reminder.\n\nHe tasted metal and dust and his own adrenaline.\n\nSecond shot.\nThird.\n\nA woman near the front went down.\nHe saw that later on replay.\n\nIn the moment, all he saw was wood splintering above his head\nand a sliver of sky\nhe was suddenly sure he might never see again.\n\n⸻\n\nIn the armored car afterward,\nhis hands were shaking.\n\nHe held them together so the agents wouldn’t see.\n\n“Are we clear?” he asked.\n\n“For now,” the lead agent said. “Shooter’s in custody. Local PD is securing the area.”\n\nHe nodded.\n\n“Any word on casualties?” he asked.\n\nThey exchanged a look.\n\n“Two confirmed injured, one critical,” the agent said. “We’ll have more soon.”\n\nHe closed his eyes.\n\nHe knew what he was supposed to think:\n\nThose poor people.\n\nWhat he actually thought was:\n\nThey missed.\n\nHe saw the bullet in his mind:\nthe arc, the angle, the calculation.\n\nHe thought of all the things that had to go wrong for it to go right,\nand all the things that had to go right for it to go wrong.\n\nWind.\nTiming.\nHis own head turning a fraction of an inch to check the teleprompter.\n\nHe should have been dead.\n\nHe was not.\n\nHe focused on that.\n\nNot the blood.\nNot the woman whose name he didn’t yet know.\nNot the family already being torn open by a phone call from a stranger.\n\nJust the simple, brute fact:\n\nI am still here.\n\n⸻\n\nBack at the secure location, the doctors fussed.\n\nNo bullet wounds. Just a graze along his ear,\na line of blood that looked dramatic on camera and trivial on the chart.\n\n“You’re very lucky,” the doctor said.\n\nCarmichael nodded.\n\nHe did not feel lucky.\n\nHe felt chosen.\n\n⸻\n\nThat night, he watched the footage.\n\nOver and over.\n\nThe slow-motion replay:\nhis head turning,\nthe flash in the distance,\nthe splinter of wood exploding inches from his face.\n\nEvery time, he thought:\n\nThere. That’s the frame where history almost ended.\n\nHe imagined the alternative timelines,\nall the ways it could have gone:\n\t•\tBullet in the throat: instant legend, chaos.\n\t•\tBullet in the chest: rushed to hospital, “fight for his life” coverage.\n\t•\tBullet in a supporter’s body (the one that actually happened): narrative wound, martyr by proxy.\n\nIn none of those timelines was he ordinary again.\n\nEven in survival, he felt the escalation.\n\nHe was no longer just a President.\nHe was a man someone had tried to erase from the story and failed.\n\nThat meant something.\n\nHe decided what.\n\n⸻\n\nIn the days that followed, the country rallied.\n\nApproval numbers shot up.\nEnemies suddenly used softer language.\nPeople who had called him a threat to democracy last month\nnow called him “Mr. President” with a catch in their throat.\n\nHe went to the hospital, cameras in tow,\nheld the hand of the woman who’d been shot in the crowd.\n\nShe tried to apologize.\n\n“I’m so sorry, Mr. President,” she whispered. “I tried to duck.”\n\nHe squeezed her hand.\n\n“No,” he said. “I’m the one who’s sorry.”\n\nHe meant it, in the small, contained way he allowed himself to mean things.\n\nBut as he walked back down the corridor,\nsurrounded by agents and staff,\nanother thought rose and pushed the apology aside:\n\nYou took a bullet meant for me.\nThat has to count for something.\n\nNot against her.\nAgainst the world.\n\n⸻\n\nThat night, alone in the residence,\nhe poured himself a drink he didn’t need\nand opened a Bible he hadn’t read in years.\n\nHe flipped it like a deck of cards,\nletting the pages slap against his thumb\nuntil it fell open somewhere in the prophets.\n\nHis eyes landed on the word anointed.\n\nIt was a translation artifact,\na word dragged across centuries and languages.\n\nHe seized it anyway.\n\nAnointed.\nChosen.\nPreserved.\n\nIf God did not want me here, he thought,\nthat bullet would have finished the job.\n\nHe wasn’t stupid.\nHe knew the physics.\nWind, angle, jittery trigger finger.\n\nBut he also knew how many men more righteous than him\nhad died in alleys, in cells, in hospital beds that smelled like mildew,\nwithout anyone naming their death a tragedy on television.\n\nIf there was no pattern, then everything was chaos.\n\nHe had spent his life refusing that conclusion.\n\nSo he did what he always did.\n\nHe made a story to sit inside.\n\nThey missed because they were meant to.\nI lived because I was supposed to.\nThis isn’t luck. It’s confirmation.\n\nConfirmation of what?\n\nHe didn’t say it out loud.\n\nHe didn’t need to.\n\n⸻\n\nWhen his advisors came to him with new security protocols,\nurging caution, a lower profile, fewer public events,\n\nhe surprised them.\n\n“We’re not hiding,” he said.\n\n“Sir, with respect, the risk—”\n\n“The risk is the proof,” he snapped.\n“They think they can scare me back into my box.\nThey think they can shoot their way out of the order I’m trying to build.”\n\nHe leaned forward.\n\n“If I step back now, I’m telling them the bullet worked.\nI’m not giving them that victory.”\n\nThe national address that night was somber, restrained, presidential.\n\nHe talked about unity.\nResilience.\nThe fragility of life.\n\nHe did not say,\n“I am the man they could not kill.”\n\nHe didn’t have to.\n\nEveryone was already thinking it.\n\n⸻\n\nIn the weeks that followed, something subtle shifted.\n\nHe became less patient with criticism.\nLess tolerant of dissent inside his own circle.\n\nWhen someone opposed a policy,\nhe no longer heard “concern.”\n\nHe heard,\n“You lived by accident, and this isn’t worth the risk you took.”\n\nAnd he could not tolerate that.\n\nIf my life was worth a stranger’s blood, he thought,\nthen my project must be worth everything.\nOtherwise I am just a man who let someone else fall in my place.\n\nThat was unacceptable.\n\nSo every new power grab, every line crossed,\nevery tool deployed that would have made his younger self sick,\n\nbecame easier to justify.\n\nThe bullet had become his theology.\n\nHe was not a man clinging to office.\n\nHe was a survivor with a mandate.\n\n⸻\n\nThis is the record of the moment\nPresident Carmichael decided\n\nthat almost dying\nmeant he was right,\n\nand that any world\nwhich disagreed with him after that\n\nwas a world\nthat deserved to be corrected.\n\n⸻",
       "isHead": true
     },
