       "body": "Scroll of Carmichael III\n\nThe Stolen Election\n\nThey called him before the numbers were final.\n\nThat, more than anything, offended him.\n\n“We’re seeing a trend, Mr. President,” the campaign manager said over speakerphone.\n“Key suburbs are underperforming. Turnout shifts in the cities. The path is… very narrow.”\n\n“Narrow isn’t closed,” Carmichael replied.\n\nA pause.\n\n“No, sir,” the manager said. “Not yet. But…”\n\nHe trailed off. Coward.\n\n“But what?” Carmichael snapped.\n\n“But the networks will probably call it within the hour,” he said.\n“We need to prepare a statement.”\n\nThere it was.\n\nThe word he’d spent his entire life outrunning:\n\nConcession.\n\n⸻\n\nHe hung up before they could say anything else.\n\nStaff would swarm. Advisers would knock.\nEveryone would want to “guide” him through this moment\nlike he was some stunned widow who needed help filling out paperwork.\n\nHe walked to the window instead.\n\nOutside, the capital glowed like nothing was wrong.\nCars moved. Streetlights held.\nSomewhere, someone was opening a bottle of cheap champagne\nbecause the numbers on their preferred channel made them feel like winners tonight.\n\nHe pressed his hand against the glass.\n\nThey think this is a swing, he thought.\nThey think history is a light switch. On one year, off the next.\n\nHe had bled too much for this job\nto believe that a handful of districts\nin states most of his voters couldn’t find on a map\nhad the right to end his story.\n\nNo.\n\nSomething was off.\n\nIf he couldn’t feel it in his gut,\nhe saw it in the data.\n\n⸻\n\nThe internal reports had been clear:\n\t•\tBase enthusiasm high.\n\t•\tOpposition fractured.\n\t•\tEconomic numbers ugly but survivable if framed correctly.\n\nHe’d hit his marks at the rallies.\nHe’d played the assassination attempt like a violin: humble, grateful, defiant.\n\nHis people loved him more, not less.\n\nSo how did he “lose”?\n\nHe flipped through the latest precinct maps.\n\nPockets of turnout in places that had been lukewarm last time.\nWeird spikes in absentee ballots.\nNumbers that didn’t match the softness he’d felt in the room at certain town halls.\n\nHe’d always trusted crowds more than charts.\n\nCrowds never lied to his face.\n\nHe remembered them:\nthe chanting, the tears, the “we love you” signs that looked homemade\neven when they weren’t.\n\nWas he supposed to believe\nthat all of that devotion dissolved in a few months\nbecause a few commentators tut-tutted on air?\n\nNo, he told himself.\nSomething else is at work.\n\nThe alternative—that they had simply grown tired of him—\nwas not admissible evidence in his personal court.\n\nSo he did what he’d trained himself to do long before he ever stepped into office:\n\nHe rewrote the charge\nso he could still play the hero.\n\n⸻\n\nThe first advisor to say the word “lose” to his face\nwas the pollster.\n\nBad choice.\n\n“Sir, the voters have sent a clear message,” the man began.\n“They—”\n\n“No,” Carmichael said, voice flat.\n“They haven’t.”\n\nThe pollster blinked.\n\n“With respect,” he tried again, “the margins—”\n\n“The margins tell me what was counted,” Carmichael said.\n“Not what was cast. Not what was suppressed. Not what was manipulated.”\n\nHe stepped closer.\n\n“Do you believe,” he asked softly,\n“that the people who chanted my name in the rain,\nwho drove six hours to stand in a field for three,\nwho told me I was the first man in years who spoke like they did—\ndo you believe those people just… changed their mind\nbecause a few pundits called me dangerous?”\n\nThe pollster faltered.\n\n“Public opinion can—”\n\n“Public opinion is a product,” Carmichael snapped.\n“They manufacture it. We’ve been manufacturing it.\nNow someone else has outbid us.”\n\nHe watched the man swallow.\n\nThere it was again.\nThe thing he refused to say out loud:\n\nMaybe they’re just tired of you.\n\nHe would not give that thought room to grow.\n\n⸻\n\nThe second advisor tried a different angle.\n\n“This could be good for your legacy,” she said.\n\nHe laughed in her face.\n\n“Do I look like a man who wants a library?” he asked.\n\nShe flushed.\n\n“What I mean,” she said carefully,\n“is that peaceful transfer of power, gracious concession—\nthose are the things history looks kindly on.”\n\n“History,” he said, “is written by people who survived long enough to own a pen.”\n\nHe poured himself a drink he didn’t need.\n\n“If I step down,” he said, “do you think the people who put me here\nwill feel peace?\nOr will they feel abandoned?”\n\nShe didn’t answer.\n\n“Exactly,” he said.\n“To them, this isn’t ‘the process working.’\nIt’s proof that the system is rigged against anyone who talks like they do.\nIf I validate that with a smile and a handshake,\nI’m telling them their enemies were right about me.\nThat I’m just another actor reading lines about unity\nwhile their towns crumble.”\n\nHe took a slow sip.\n\n“Do you want to be the one to explain that to them?” he asked.\n\nShe looked away.\n\nHe’d won that argument.\nNot because he was right,\nbut because he understood which fear to touch.\n\n⸻\n\nIt didn’t take long for the stories to start.\n\nWhispers from county officials.\nVideos of “glitches” in late-night counts.\nScreenshots of tallies that went up and down in ways\nthat looked sinister to people who lived on a diet of outrage.\n\nMost of it was noise.\n\nHe didn’t care.\n\nHe cared that it gave him a word\nhe could use instead of “defeat.”\n\nStolen.\n\nStolen meant:\n\t•\tHe was still wanted.\n\t•\tHe was still chosen.\n\t•\tThe problem wasn’t him.\n\nStolen meant:\n\t•\tAn enemy to name,\n\t•\tA wrong to right,\n\t•\tA war to fight.\n\nHe could work with that.\n\nHe started small.\n\n“We’re looking into irregularities,” he said on camera.\n“Every legal vote should be counted.”\n\nReasonable. Presidential.\n\nBehind closed doors, his language sharpened.\n\n“They’re taking it from us,” he told donors and loyalists.\n“They can’t beat us at the ballot box, so they’re doing it in the dark.”\n\nHe watched the phrase “in the dark” catch fire in their eyes.\n\nDark meant them.\nThem meant everyone who’d ever made his voters feel stupid, small, or obsolete.\n\nHe whispered to that wound like a lover.\n\n⸻\n\nHis lawyers told him the cases were weak.\n\nHe knew.\n\nHe didn’t need the courts to reverse the outcome.\n\nHe needed the fight itself\nto overwrite the loss.\n\nIf enough people believed the election was illegitimate,\nthen the man sworn in after him\nwould sit on a cracked throne.\n\nHe might hold the office.\nCarmichael would hold the grievance.\n\nAnd in this country, grievance traveled farther than law.\n\n⸻\n\nThe Baptist—back when he still spoke to him as a man—\nhad once warned him about this.\n\n“Resentment is a sacrament now,” the Baptist had said.\n“People take it like communion.\nYou start feeding them that as your main diet,\nthey’ll drink poison if you bless it.”\n\nAt the time, Carmichael had rolled his eyes.\n\nNow, staring at the screen as crowds chanted “Stop the Steal”\nin cities across the map,\nhe realized the Baptist had been half right.\n\nResentment wasn’t just a sacrament.\n\nIt was fuel.\n\nAnd he had more of it in storage\nthan any of his rivals.\n\n⸻\n\nHis staff drafted two speeches.\n\nOne was concession:\n\t•\tCall for unity.\n\t•\tAcknowledge “the will of the people.”\n\t•\tPledge smooth transfer, graceful exit.\n\nThe other was something else:\n\t•\tLegal challenges.\n\t•\t“Serious concerns.”\n\t•\t“We owe it to our supporters to exhaust every avenue.”\n\nHe read both.\n\nThe concession speech felt like a eulogy\nfor someone he didn’t know.\n\nThe second felt like a to-do list.\n\n“Use this one,” he said, tapping the page with the “concerns.”\n\n“Sir,” the chief of staff said carefully,\n“there is a real risk of violence if we push this too hard.”\n\n“Violence from whom?” he asked.\n\n“Your supporters,” she said. “They trust you. If you tell them—”\n\n“If they act,” he interrupted, “it will be because they have been pushed to the edge for years.\nI didn’t load that spring.\nI just pointed to it.”\n\n“That’s not how they’ll see it on the news,” she said.\n\nHe smiled thinly.\n\n“Since when has the news ever seen me clearly?” he asked.\n\n⸻\n\nHe went before the cameras,\nthe mask perfectly in place.\n\nHe did not call for calm.\n\nHe called for vigilance.\n\nHe did not bless the outcome.\n\nHe blessed the suspicion.\n\nHe didn’t explicitly say the words “stolen election.”\nHe didn’t have to.\n\nHe just asked, over and over:\n\n“Does this feel right to you?”\n\nHe knew the answer in their bones would be no,\nbecause their lives hadn’t felt right in years,\nand nobody powerful had ever told them\nthat their discomfort counted as data.\n\nHe offered them that validation now,\nat scale.\n\nNot because he intended to fix their lives,\nbut because their pain was the only currency\nthat could buy him another chapter.\n\n⸻\n\nIn the weeks that followed,\nas courts dismissed case after case,\nas fact-checkers debunked claim after claim,\n\nhe felt something harden inside him.\n\nIt wasn’t grief.\nHe didn’t allow himself that.\n\nIt was contempt.\n\nFor the judges who refused to “see the bigger picture.”\nFor the reporters who treated his supporters like a pathology.\nFor the colleagues who urged him in private to concede\nand then went on TV to praise “our resilient democracy”\nwhile his people filled the streets.\n\nHe told himself they weren’t just attacking him.\n\nThey were attacking the country that had chosen him.\n\nTherefore any blow he landed in response\nwas an act of defense.\n\nSelf-defense.\nNational defense.\nThe lines blurred pleasantly.\n\n⸻\n\nHe began talking about “enemies of the people” more freely.\n\nHe named departments.\nThen agencies.\nThen individuals.\n\n“Not everyone who opposes me is an enemy,” he said in one interview.\n“But every enemy this country faces\nseems strangely aligned against me.”\n\nHe watched that line clip itself into a meme.\n\nHe didn’t care if it was logically sound.\n\nHe cared that it felt true\nto millions of people\nwho had decided their fate and his\nwere braided together.\n\n⸻\n\nPrivately, he made himself a promise:\n\nI may have to leave the building.\nBut I will not leave the stage.\n\nHe would do his term,\nperform the rituals,\nstep aside when forced to.\n\nBut only in the most technical sense.\n\nHe would not bless the man who came after him.\nHe would not frame this as “the will of the people.”\nHe would frame it as the will of the machine—\n\t•\tThe media machine,\n\t•\tThe bureaucratic machine,\n\t•\tThe voting system machine,\n\t•\tThe foreign influence machine,\n\nall of which, he now believed with near-religious certainty,\nhad converged for one purpose:\n\nTo remove him from the place God and the people\nhad clearly intended him to occupy.\n\nDid he have proof of that?\n\nNot the kind a court would accept.\n\nBut he had a bullet scar and a crowd that still chanted his name.\n\nFor him, that was evidence enough.\n\n⸻\n\nThis is the record of the season\n\nwhen President Carmichael decided\n\nthat losing an election\ncould not mean he had been rejected,\n\nonly that destiny had been interrupted\n\nby thieves\nwho now needed to be found,\nnamed,\n\nand punished.",
       "isHead": true
     },
