+      "body": "Scene: Sandi Hears It\n\nSandi heard it in a place no one could film.\n\nNot a rally. Not a studio.\nA kitchen with a single lamp on, the rest of the apartment dim. A glass of water sweating on the counter. A pill organizer open like a quiet confession.\n\nHer phone was face-down when the first message came in.\n\nShe didn’t pick it up.\n\nShe already knew what the world was doing with her body. She refused to check the scoreboard like it was her job to witness her own humiliation.\n\nThen the second message came—from someone she didn’t ignore.\n\nHer friend. Her only real friend from before the palace, before the handlers, before her last name became a weapon.\n\nHave you heard this.\nPlease tell me it’s not real.\n\nSandi flipped the phone over.\n\nA link.\n\nA thumbnail of a waveform and the Baptist’s face from an old photo—grainy, sacred-looking, the kind of image people use when they want authority without accountability.\n\nText above it:\n\nBAPTIST URGES CAUTION ABOUT ONE. “TRUST INSTITUTIONS.”\n\nSandi stared at the words until they turned flat.\n\nThen she pressed play.\n\nThe voice came through clean, steady, tender.\n\nFor a heartbeat, she felt relief—\nthe primitive kind, the kind your body gives you when a missing person seems to reappear.\n\nThen the content arrived.\n\n“Trust established institutions…”\n“Stability…”\n“Responsibility…”\nSoft phrases. Smooth edges.\n\nSandi’s face didn’t change, but something behind her eyes sharpened.\n\nBecause she had heard her father use those words.\n\nNot as meaning—\nas framing.\n\nShe listened to the full clip once. Then again.\n\nNot to “judge.”\n\nTo detect.\n\nShe grew up inside media training. She grew up inside rooms where statements were built like furniture—polished, sanded, varnished, delivered.\n\nShe knew the difference between a man speaking and a machine delivering.\n\nThis voice was almost perfect.\n\nThat was the problem.\n\nIt contained no risk.\nNo witness.\nNo grit.\n\nIt had the cleanliness of something that had never paid a price.\n\nHer phone buzzed again.\n\nA second clip, “newly surfaced.”\n\nSame voice. Same calm.\n\nSame… obedience.\n\nSandi set the phone down gently, as if it were fragile.\n\nShe stood up and walked to the window.\n\nThe city was doing what it always did: living anyway.\n\nA couple argued on the sidewalk. A dog dragged its owner toward a tree. A teenager laughed too loudly at nothing.\n\nSandi put her hand on the glass.\n\nShe whispered, almost to herself:\n\n“That’s not him.”\n\nShe didn’t say it like a theory.\nShe said it like a recognition.\n\nHer phone buzzed again.\n\nThis time it was an unknown number.\n\nShe almost didn’t answer.\n\nAlmost.\n\nThen she did, because some part of her wanted the pain clean.\n\n“Hello,” she said.\n\nHer father’s voice came through warm.\n\n“Sweetheart.”\n\nSandi closed her eyes.\n\n“What did you do,” she asked.\n\nA pause.\n\nHer father chuckled softly, like she’d misunderstood something simple.\n\n“I’m managing a crisis,” he said.\n\n“No,” Sandi replied. “You’re rewriting a person.”\n\nAnother pause. Longer.\n\n“You’re emotional,” he said gently. “You’re under stress.”\n\nSandi’s hand tightened on the phone.\n\n“I know what stress is,” she said. “I grew up with you.”\n\nSilence.\n\nHe sighed as if tired of being accused.\n\n“You don’t understand the stakes,” he said.\n\nSandi’s voice stayed quiet.\n\n“I understand the line.”\n\nHer father’s tone cooled by a degree.\n\n“What line.”\n\nSandi turned away from the window. Walked back to the table. Looked at the glass of water, the pill organizer, the small domestic proof that she was a human being and not a headline.\n\n“You can attack One,” she said. “That’s politics. That’s ugly, but it’s your world.”\n\nHer voice steadied.\n\n“But you don’t get to kidnap people into your story. You don’t get to steal a man’s voice.”\n\nHer father exhaled slowly.\n\n“You’re making assumptions,” he said.\n\nSandi’s mouth tightened.\n\n“I’m making recognition.”\n\nAnother pause.\n\nThen her father spoke carefully, like he was stepping over broken glass.\n\n“Do you know what it means,” he said, “when a nation starts trusting prophets more than institutions?”\n\nSandi laughed once—small, incredulous.\n\n“You mean when people stop trusting you.”\n\nHer father’s voice hardened.\n\n“Watch your tone.”\n\nSandi’s voice didn’t rise. It dropped.\n\n“Watch your soul,” she said.\n\nSilence hit like a door closing.\n\nThen her father, quieter now:\n\n“You’re pregnant,” he said, as if it were a lever he could pull. “You need protection.”\n\nSandi’s eyes flashed.\n\n“Protection from who,” she asked, “you?”\n\nHe didn’t answer.\n\nThat was answer enough.\n\nSandi’s hand went to her stomach—instinctive, fierce.\n\nShe spoke slowly, each word chosen like a vow.\n\n“If you took him,” she said, “bring him back.”\n\nHer father’s voice softened again, practiced softness.\n\n“Sweetheart—”\n\n“Bring him back,” she repeated.\n\nA pause.\n\nThen her father, almost kind:\n\n“You’re not in a position to make demands.”\n\nSandi’s face went still.\n\n“Yes,” she said. “I am.”\n\nHe scoffed lightly. “How.”\n\nSandi looked at the fake waveform on her screen.\n\nThen she said the sentence that changed the shape of the war:\n\n“I’m going to tell the truth,” she said. “And you can’t arrest me without confessing what you are.”\n\nHer father went silent.\n\nFor the first time, she heard something underneath him—\nnot anger.\n\nFear.\n\nHe spoke again, controlled.\n\n“Be careful,” he said. “People get hurt.”\n\nSandi didn’t flinch.\n\n“People are already hurt,” she said. “You just call it stability.”\n\nShe ended the call.\n\nNo speech. No post. No spectacle.\n\nJust a decision.\n\nShe picked up her phone and opened a draft message—not to the public.\n\nTo One.\n\nThree lines only.\n\nIt’s fake.\nThey took him.\nWe build the test.\n\nShe stared at the message for a moment before sending.\n\nThen she pressed send.\n\nAnd in the quiet kitchen, the Fourth Branch culture appeared again—not as an institution yet, but as a practice:\n\nRestraint.\nVerification.\nRefusal.\n\nA way of being human that the empire could not monetize.",
+      "isHead": true
+    },
