       "body": "SCENE_28_THE_HAITIAN_NETWORK.md\n\nScene: The Haitian Network\n\nThey didn’t call it a network.\n\nNetworks sound like strategy.\nThis was older than strategy.\n\nIt was kinship.\n\nThe woman who opened the door—Auntie Jo—wasn’t an aunt by blood. She was an aunt by history. A woman who had cooked for three different families and raised half the block with discipline and rice.\n\nShe looked at Sandi like she was both fragile and stubborn.\n\n“Sit,” she said. “Eat.”\n\nSandi tried to refuse. “I’m okay—”\n\nAuntie Jo’s eyes narrowed.\n\n“You’re carrying life,” she said. “You don’t argue with soup.”\n\nOne almost smiled, but didn’t.\n\nHe understood immediately what this was:\n\nNot charity.\n\nProtection with dignity.\n\nThey kept the radio on low—not for news, for noise. A cheap Haitian station with a DJ who talked too much and played Kompa like it was medicine.\n\nNeighbors came in and out without looking curious. That was the first rule.\n\nCuriosity is an invitation.\nRespect is a wall.\n\nAuntie Jo’s nephew—tall, quiet—installed an old router and set up a local-only drive share so they could store copies without cloud exposure.\n\nNo speeches. No “I’m honored.”\nJust work.\n\nA cousin brought groceries at odd hours.\nA friend of a friend drove them once, no questions.\nA barber down the street offered the back room if they ever needed a temporary hide.\n\nNone of it was announced. None of it was posted.\n\nAnd that’s why it worked.\n\nOutside, the slur #HaitianBlood kept being flung around online like a dirty coin.\n\nInside this apartment, Haitian blood meant something else:\n\nIt meant people who had survived governments that “disappeared” men.\n\nPeople who didn’t need to be convinced that proof could be edited, or that officials could lie with perfect confidence.\n\nAuntie Jo sat at the table one night while One labeled a drive.\n\nShe watched him for a long time, then spoke.\n\n“You know why they hate you?” she asked.\n\nOne didn’t look up. “Because I won’t perform.”\n\nAuntie Jo nodded once.\n\n“Yes,” she said. “And because you remind them that poor people can see.”\n\nOne paused.\n\nSandi sat quietly, listening.\n\nAuntie Jo continued, voice steady, not dramatic.\n\n“In Haiti, we learned early,” she said. “The man on the radio is not the man in the street. The man on the TV is not the man who knocked at your door.”\n\nShe pointed gently at the hard drives.\n\n“So you do right,” she said. “You keep the originals.”\n\nSandi swallowed.\n\n“My father will say they’re hiding me,” she said quietly.\n\nAuntie Jo snorted.\n\n“Let him talk,” she said. “Talking is how cowards fight.”\n\nOne looked up, surprised.\n\nAuntie Jo met his eyes.\n\n“You want to protect the baby?” she said.\n\nSandi’s hand went to her belly.\n\nAuntie Jo nodded.\n\n“Then you keep your circle small,” she said. “And you keep your truth clean.”\n\nOne exhaled slowly.\n\nThe regime had money.\n\nIt had cameras.\n\nIt had platforms.\n\nBut it didn’t have this:\n\nA community that didn’t need likes to stay loyal.\n\nThat night, a message came through One’s burner phone—one of the librarian contacts.\n\nDecoy room pushed hard. People confused. Need a calm method response.\n\nOne read it.\n\nSandi looked at him.\n\n“What,” she asked.\n\nOne’s voice stayed steady.\n\n“They built a fake room,” he said. “To make verification look like a cult.”\n\nAuntie Jo laughed, sharp.\n\n“Of course,” she said. “That’s what they do.”\n\nSandi’s eyes hardened.\n\n“We respond with a checklist,” she said.\n\nOne nodded.\n\nAuntie Jo stood.\n\nShe placed a bowl of soup in front of Sandi like it was a decree.\n\n“And you respond,” she said, “after you eat.”\n\nSandi almost smiled.\n\nOne felt something shift in his chest.\n\nNot hope exactly.\n\nSomething sturdier:\n\nA reminder that the machine was not the whole world.\n\nThe world also contained kitchens.\n\nHands.\n\nSoup.\n\nPeople who understood that restraint is not weakness—\nit is a kind of strength that doesn’t need permission.\n\n⸻",
       "isHead": true
     },
+    {
+      "id": "scene_29_the_baptist_sends_a_sign",
+      "title": "Scene 29 — The Baptist Sends a Sign",
+      "series": "Scenes",
+      "kind": "scene",
+      "who": [
+        "Baptist"
+      ],
+      "status": "canon",
+      "arc": 4,
+      "order": 29,
+      "summary": "Held captive, the Baptist survives interrogation through boredom and refuses to give his captors a usable narrative, planting the physical signal that later exposes the edits.",
+      "supersedes": null,
+      "visibility": "public",
+      "file": "SCENE_29_THE_BAPTIST_SENDS_A_SIGN.md",
+      "body": "Scene: The Sign\n\nThey underestimated boredom.\n\nThat’s what kept the Baptist alive.\n\nNot heroism. Not rage.\nBoredom—the ability to sit inside a cage without giving the captors a story.\n\nDays blurred into procedures.\n\nLights on. Lights off.\nMeals that tasted like compliance.\nQuestions asked by people who pretended to care.\n\n“Do you feel safe?”\n“Do you feel coerced?”\n“Do you understand the importance of stability?”\n\nHe answered with the same calm sentence every time:\n\n“I understand what you are doing.”\n\nThey tried flattery.\n\n“You could help guide the nation,” the suit said one day.\n\nThe Baptist stared at him.\n\n“You mean I could help guide your narrative,” he replied.\n\nThey tried fear.\n\n“People get hurt,” the suit said.\n\nThe Baptist nodded once.\n\n“People get hurt when liars need proof,” he said.\n\nThey tried exhaustion.\n\nBut boredom was stronger than exhaustion.\n\nBecause boredom had a secret:\n\nIt kept his mind free.\n\nOne night—late—an intern came to adjust equipment in the studio room. Young, nervous, avoiding eye contact like the building itself was watching her.\n\nShe dropped a pen.\n\nThe Baptist saw her hands shake when she picked it up.\n\nHe said softly, not unkind:\n\n“You don’t like this.”\n\nThe intern froze.\n\nHer eyes flicked up, then away.\n\n“You shouldn’t talk to me,” she whispered.\n\nThe Baptist waited.\n\nThe intern swallowed.\n\n“They said you’re dangerous,” she said.\n\nThe Baptist nodded.\n\n“They say that about anyone who asks for proof,” he replied.\n\nThe intern’s throat moved. “They said you were free.”\n\nThe Baptist looked at her carefully.\n\n“If I were free,” he said, “would you be whispering?”\n\nThe intern’s eyes watered. Just a little.\n\nHe didn’t push. He didn’t preach.\n\nHe asked one question, calm as a door opening:\n\n“Do you know what a checksum is,” he said.\n\nShe blinked. “A… hash?”\n\nThe Baptist nodded.\n\n“Good,” he said. “Then you understand why I need something you can’t edit.”\n\nThe intern stared at him, breathing shallow.\n\n“What,” she whispered.\n\nThe Baptist leaned forward slightly.\n\n“Tomorrow,” he said, “they’ll record another ‘proof’ clip. They will cut what they don’t like. That’s how they live.”\n\nHe paused.\n\n“So I’m going to give you an ordinary detail,” he said. “A detail so strange and specific that it can’t be guessed, only witnessed.”\n\nThe intern’s eyes widened.\n\n“And if that detail disappears,” he said, “people will know.”\n\nShe swallowed hard.\n\n“I can’t—”\n\n“I’m not asking you to fight them,” the Baptist said quietly. “I’m asking you to do one thing that still makes you human.”\n\nThe intern’s lips trembled.\n\n“What.”\n\nThe Baptist spoke with deliberate slowness, like he was carving a mark into stone.\n\n“During the recording,” he said, “I will lift my left hand. I will tap my ring finger three times.”\n\nThe intern frowned.\n\n“That’s—”\n\n“A signal,” the Baptist said. “Not a phrase. Not a word they can steal.”\n\nHe watched her.\n\n“And after the recording,” he continued, “you will upload the raw file—just once—to an address I will give you. An archive address. One that does not monetize truth.”\n\nThe intern stared at him like he’d handed her a knife.\n\n“I’ll get caught,” she whispered.\n\nThe Baptist nodded.\n\n“Yes,” he said. “That’s why you will do it like a ghost.”\n\nHe leaned back.\n\n“And if you can’t upload the raw,” he added gently, “then at least remember this: left hand, ring finger, three taps.”\n\nThe intern’s eyes glistened.\n\n“Why me,” she whispered.\n\nThe Baptist’s voice stayed calm.\n\n“Because you’re still young enough to feel sick,” he said. “That means your conscience is still alive.”\n\nA long silence.\n\nThen the intern gave a tiny nod.\n\nNot brave.\n\nJust real.\n\nThe next day, they rolled camera again.\n\nThe suit watched from off-frame like always.\n\n“Keep it clean,” he warned.\n\nThe Baptist looked into the lens.\n\n“My name is Baptiste,” he said. “And I am alive.”\n\nHalfway through, as he spoke about verification, he lifted his left hand—casual, as if adjusting his cuff.\n\nAnd he tapped his ring finger three times.\n\nOnce.\nTwice.\nThree.\n\nSo subtle most people would miss it.\n\nBut the careful people—now trained—would watch.\n\nThe suit didn’t notice.\n\nHe was listening for words, not bodies.\n\nAfter the recording, as the technicians moved around, the intern’s hand hovered near the console.\n\nHer heart hammered.\n\nShe slipped a USB into a port like she was simply doing her job.\n\nCopy.\nEject.\nHide.\n\nA tiny act.\n\nNot rebellion with fireworks.\n\nRebellion with file transfer.\n\nThat night, somewhere far from the facility, a librarian’s secure inbox received a file.\n\nSubject line:\n\nRAW — PROOF\n\nNo message.\n\nJust an attachment.\n\nAnd in a room lit by a soft lamp, One stared at the clip frame by frame until he saw it—\n\nthe ring finger taps—\n\nand he whispered, like a man hearing a friend through a wall:\n\n“He’s alive.”\n\nSandi exhaled, eyes wet.\n\nThe Haitian auntie crossed herself without asking permission.\n\nAnd in that moment, the regime’s greatest weakness revealed itself again:\n\nIt could counterfeit voices.\n\nBut it could not fully counterfeit witness.",
+      "isHead": true
+    },
     {
       "id": "scene_30_the_intern_disappears",
       "title": "Scene 30 — The Intern Disappears",
