-      "body": "SCENE_30_THE_INTERN_DISAPPEARS.md\n\nScene: The Intern Disappears\n\nShe didn’t sleep.\n\nShe lay on her back in a room that felt suddenly too small, listening to the building creak like it was talking about her.\n\nThe USB was gone—hidden where she’d hidden things as a kid: inside a hollowed book spine, taped shut, slid behind a row of other books so it looked like nothing.\n\nShe kept replaying the moment her hand touched the console.\n\nCopy.\nEject.\nHide.\n\nIt had felt like nothing.\n\nNow it felt like a siren that only she could hear.\n\nAt 6:04 AM, her phone lit up.\n\nUnknown number.\n\nNo text. Just an image.\n\nA still frame of her face from the studio camera—sharp, zoomed, time-stamped.\n\nBeneath it, one sentence:\n\nDON’T DO THAT AGAIN.\n\nHer stomach dropped.\n\nShe stared at it until her eyes burned.\n\nThen her phone buzzed again—this time a calendar notification she didn’t recognize:\n\nMeeting — 8:30 AM — Compliance Review\n\nNo location. No details.\n\nJust certainty.\n\nShe sat up slowly, heart loud.\n\nIn the bathroom mirror, she looked like someone who’d been crying without tears.\n\nShe brushed her teeth anyway, hands shaking.\n\nAt 7:50 she left her apartment.\n\nNot because she thought she could hide—\nbecause if she didn’t show up, they’d come anyway.\n\nOutside, the air was too cold. The street too normal.\n\nA black sedan idled across from her building.\n\nNo flashing lights. No badges.\n\nJust a car that knew where she lived.\n\nShe walked.\n\nThe sedan rolled behind her at a respectful distance, like a shadow pretending to be polite.\n\nAt 8:12, she turned a corner and the sedan turned too.\n\nAt 8:20, a second car appeared ahead of her—parked, engine running.\n\nHer throat tightened.\n\nTwo cars was a message:\n\nWe don’t need to chase you.\nWe’ve already decided.\n\nAt 8:27, her phone buzzed again.\n\nA text:\n\nTURN LEFT.\n\nShe froze mid-step.\n\nPeople flowed around her—commuters, headphones, coffee cups.\n\nNo one noticed.\n\nThat was the point.\n\nShe turned left.\n\nAt 8:29, another text:\n\nDO NOT RUN.\n\nHer knees felt watery.\n\nShe walked.\n\nAt 8:31, she reached a door she’d never noticed before—plain, industrial, unmarked.\n\nThe first car pulled to the curb.\n\nA man got out—no uniform, no expression—opened the door and gestured politely as if she’d been invited.\n\n“Morning,” he said.\n\nHer voice came out thin.\n\n“Am I in trouble?”\n\nThe man smiled like a human resources rep.\n\n“You’re in review,” he said.\n\nShe wanted to scream.\n\nInstead she stepped inside.\n\nThe door closed behind her with a sound that was too soft to be mercy.\n\nDown a hallway, fluorescent lights hummed.\n\nAt the end, a room with a table and two chairs.\n\nThe suit was there.\n\nHe smiled like an old friend.\n\n“Thank you for coming,” he said.\n\nShe sat without being told.\n\nHer hands clenched in her lap to keep them from shaking.\n\nThe suit folded his hands.\n\n“You’ve been under pressure,” he said gently. “It’s understandable.”\n\nShe said nothing.\n\nThe suit nodded, as if her silence was an admission.\n\n“We’re not here to punish you,” he said. “We’re here to protect you.”\n\nShe stared at him.\n\n“From what,” she whispered.\n\nThe suit’s smile thinned.\n\n“From the kind of mistakes that ruin young lives,” he said.\n\nHe slid a folder across the table.\n\nInside: screenshots, logs, access timestamps.\n\nHer name in multiple columns.\n\nThe suit tapped the page lightly.\n\n“You accessed material outside your role,” he said. “You shared it.”\n\nHer throat tightened.\n\n“I didn’t—”\n\nThe suit held up a hand, calm.\n\n“Don’t,” he said. “Lying is the part that makes this worse.”\n\nShe felt her chest compress.\n\n“What happens now,” she asked.\n\nThe suit leaned back.\n\n“Now,” he said, “you take a break.”\n\nShe blinked.\n\n“A break?”\n\nThe suit nodded.\n\n“Administrative leave,” he said. “Your accounts will be suspended. Your devices will be held for review.”\n\nHer breath caught.\n\n“And if I don’t agree,” she whispered.\n\nThe suit’s voice stayed gentle.\n\n“Then we reclassify what you did,” he said. “And it becomes criminal.”\n\nSilence.\n\nShe understood then: there was no path through this room that didn’t lead where they wanted.\n\nThe suit slid a pen toward her.\n\n“Sign,” he said.\n\nHer hand hovered.\n\nHer conscience screamed.\n\nHer fear answered louder.\n\nShe signed.\n\nThe suit took the paper, nodded with practiced satisfaction.\n\n“Good,” he said. “Now. One more thing.”\n\nShe looked up.\n\nThe suit’s eyes sharpened.\n\n“You will not contact anyone,” he said. “You will not post anything. You will not speak to press.”\n\nHe paused.\n\n“And you will forget,” he said softly, “that you ever saw a ring finger tap.”\n\nHer stomach turned.\n\nShe stood slowly on legs that didn’t feel like hers.\n\nThe suit smiled again.\n\n“We’ll drive you home,” he said.\n\nShe blinked.\n\n“I can walk.”\n\nThe suit’s smile didn’t change.\n\n“No,” he said. “You can’t.”\n\n⸻\n\nThat afternoon, her name vanished.\n\nHer accounts logged out everywhere.\nHer badge didn’t work.\nHer email bounced.\n\nEven her friends’ messages to her began to fail—sent, but never delivered.\n\nIt wasn’t a dramatic disappearance.\n\nIt was worse.\n\nA soft deletion.\n\nAnd in the room with the lamp and the Haitian radio humming low, One got a message from the librarian contact:\n\nRaw file received. Source went dark immediately after. Likely compromised.\n\nSandi’s face went pale.\n\n“We have to protect her,” she said.\n\nOne stared at the message.\n\nHe felt the familiar fire rise—the desire to react, to rage, to burn the feed down with truth.\n\nThen he remembered the rule.\n\nDon’t chase the feed.\n\nBuild the test.\n\nHe looked at Sandi.\n\n“We can’t save her with noise,” he said.\n\nSandi’s eyes hardened.\n\n“Then how.”\n\nOne’s voice was steady.\n\n“We make her disappearance visible without naming her,” he said. “We force the regime to choose: admit they control edits… or admit they punish witnesses.”\n\nSandi exhaled sharply.\n\n“Release the raw,” she said.\n\nOne nodded.\n\n“But strategically,” he replied. “So it protects her, not exposes her.”\n\n⸻\n\nSCENE_31_THE_COUNTERMOVE.md\n\nScene: The Countermove\n\nThey waited exactly twelve hours.\n\nNot because they were afraid.\n\nBecause timing is protection.\n\nIf they released the raw clip immediately, the regime could trace the leak by proximity.\n\nIf they waited, the trail widened.\n\nIn the new room, One and Sandi prepared the package like surgeons.\n\nNo names.\nNo speculation.\nNo mythology.\n\nJust proof.\n\nThey created three assets:\n\t1.\tThe raw Baptist clip with checksum and file hash\n\t2.\tA frame-by-frame highlight of the ring-finger taps\n\t3.\tA timeline showing the original upload, the edited reposts, and the moments of removal\n\nThey sent it to:\n\t•\tthe librarian archive\n\t•\tthe investigative journalist collective\n\t•\ttwo mainstream outlets who had already shown discipline\n\nAnd then they posted publicly—not the raw file itself, but a plain statement:\n\nA raw, hash-verified version of the Baptist recording exists.\nIt includes a physical signal (three taps) that is missing from edited versions now circulating.\nAny outlet can request the file and verify the hash.\nWe will not reveal sources. We will not encourage harassment.\nWe will document edits.\n\nThen, quietly, embedded in ordinary language:\n\nTruth survives in the kitchen light.\n\nThe response was immediate, but not hysterical.\n\nThe careful people moved first.\n\nThey asked for the hash.\n\nThey confirmed the taps.\n\nThey compared the edits.\n\nThen the larger press—forced by the integrity of the package—began to move too.\n\nA mainstream anchor held up a printed still frame and said:\n\n“If this is authentic, it suggests not just manipulation—but suppression.”\n\nThe regime now faced a dilemma it hated:\n\t•\tIf they denied the raw clip, they looked like liars against math.\n\t•\tIf they admitted it, they admitted edits.\n\t•\tIf they attacked the source, they confessed coercion.\n\nIn the White House, the President watched the segment.\n\nThe Chief of Staff spoke carefully.\n\n“Sir, this is escalating into—”\n\nThe President cut him off.\n\n“I know what it is,” he said softly.\n\nHe turned to the security advisor.\n\n“Did we lose the source,” he asked.\n\nThe advisor hesitated. “We contained her.”\n\nThe President’s eyes narrowed.\n\n“Contained,” he repeated, amused. “You mean you deleted her.”\n\nThe advisor stiffened.\n\nThe President nodded.\n\n“Good,” he said. “Now we do the next move.”\n\nThe Chief of Staff frowned.\n\n“What next move.”\n\nThe President’s voice dropped.\n\n“We release our own raw,” he said. “A new one. Cleaner. With taps.”\n\nThe director blinked. “Sir, if we mimic the taps—”\n\nThe President raised a hand.\n\n“Then we make taps meaningless,” he said. “We flood the test until the public is tired.”\n\nHe leaned forward.\n\n“Truth cannot compete with infinite noise,” he said. “So we don’t compete. We drown.”\n\nThe room went quiet.\n\nBecause the plan was clear:\n\nNot to win an argument.\n\nTo exhaust the citizenry until verification felt impossible.\n\nBack in the small room with the lamp, One stared at the wall for a long time.\n\nSandi watched him.\n\n“They’re going to flood it,” she said.\n\nOne nodded.\n\n“Yes,” he replied.\n\nSandi’s jaw tightened.\n\n“So what do we do when they flood the test.”\n\nOne’s voice was calm.\n\n“We evolve it,” he said. “We make it human again. Something you can’t mass-produce.”\n\nSandi’s hand went to her stomach.\n\n“What.”\n\nOne looked at her.\n\nAnd said the next hard truth:\n\n“We stop relying on signals that live only in media,” he said. “We build living witnesses. In-person verification. Community chains.”\n\nSandi exhaled.\n\n“The Haitian network,” she said.\n\n“One network,” One replied. “Then many.”\n\nAnd in that moment, the conflict widened from a story about one couple and one kidnapped mentor—\n\nto a civilizational question:\n\nCan a society maintain reality\nwhen power can generate infinite counterfeits?\n\n⸻",
+      "body": "SCENE_30_THE_INTERN_DISAPPEARS.md\n\nScene: The Intern Disappears\n\nShe didn’t sleep.\n\nShe lay on her back in a room that felt suddenly too small, listening to the building creak like it was talking about her.\n\nThe USB was gone—hidden where she’d hidden things as a kid: inside a hollowed book spine, taped shut, slid behind a row of other books so it looked like nothing.\n\nShe kept replaying the moment her hand touched the console.\n\nCopy.\nEject.\nHide.\n\nIt had felt like nothing.\n\nNow it felt like a siren that only she could hear.\n\nAt 6:04 AM, her phone lit up.\n\nUnknown number.\n\nNo text. Just an image.\n\nA still frame of her face from the studio camera—sharp, zoomed, time-stamped.\n\nBeneath it, one sentence:\n\nDON’T DO THAT AGAIN.\n\nHer stomach dropped.\n\nShe stared at it until her eyes burned.\n\nThen her phone buzzed again—this time a calendar notification she didn’t recognize:\n\nMeeting — 8:30 AM — Compliance Review\n\nNo location. No details.\n\nJust certainty.\n\nShe sat up slowly, heart loud.\n\nIn the bathroom mirror, she looked like someone who’d been crying without tears.\n\nShe brushed her teeth anyway, hands shaking.\n\nAt 7:50 she left her apartment.\n\nNot because she thought she could hide—\nbecause if she didn’t show up, they’d come anyway.\n\nOutside, the air was too cold. The street too normal.\n\nA black sedan idled across from her building.\n\nNo flashing lights. No badges.\n\nJust a car that knew where she lived.\n\nShe walked.\n\nThe sedan rolled behind her at a respectful distance, like a shadow pretending to be polite.\n\nAt 8:12, she turned a corner and the sedan turned too.\n\nAt 8:20, a second car appeared ahead of her—parked, engine running.\n\nHer throat tightened.\n\nTwo cars was a message:\n\nWe don’t need to chase you.\nWe’ve already decided.\n\nAt 8:27, her phone buzzed again.\n\nA text:\n\nTURN LEFT.\n\nShe froze mid-step.\n\nPeople flowed around her—commuters, headphones, coffee cups.\n\nNo one noticed.\n\nThat was the point.\n\nShe turned left.\n\nAt 8:29, another text:\n\nDO NOT RUN.\n\nHer knees felt watery.\n\nShe walked.\n\nAt 8:31, she reached a door she’d never noticed before—plain, industrial, unmarked.\n\nThe first car pulled to the curb.\n\nA man got out—no uniform, no expression—opened the door and gestured politely as if she’d been invited.\n\n“Morning,” he said.\n\nHer voice came out thin.\n\n“Am I in trouble?”\n\nThe man smiled like a human resources rep.\n\n“You’re in review,” he said.\n\nShe wanted to scream.\n\nInstead she stepped inside.\n\nThe door closed behind her with a sound that was too soft to be mercy.\n\nDown a hallway, fluorescent lights hummed.\n\nAt the end, a room with a table and two chairs.\n\nThe suit was there.\n\nHe smiled like an old friend.\n\n“Thank you for coming,” he said.\n\nShe sat without being told.\n\nHer hands clenched in her lap to keep them from shaking.\n\nThe suit folded his hands.\n\n“You’ve been under pressure,” he said gently. “It’s understandable.”\n\nShe said nothing.\n\nThe suit nodded, as if her silence was an admission.\n\n“We’re not here to punish you,” he said. “We’re here to protect you.”\n\nShe stared at him.\n\n“From what,” she whispered.\n\nThe suit’s smile thinned.\n\n“From the kind of mistakes that ruin young lives,” he said.\n\nHe slid a folder across the table.\n\nInside: screenshots, logs, access timestamps.\n\nHer name in multiple columns.\n\nThe suit tapped the page lightly.\n\n“You accessed material outside your role,” he said. “You shared it.”\n\nHer throat tightened.\n\n“I didn’t—”\n\nThe suit held up a hand, calm.\n\n“Don’t,” he said. “Lying is the part that makes this worse.”\n\nShe felt her chest compress.\n\n“What happens now,” she asked.\n\nThe suit leaned back.\n\n“Now,” he said, “you take a break.”\n\nShe blinked.\n\n“A break?”\n\nThe suit nodded.\n\n“Administrative leave,” he said. “Your accounts will be suspended. Your devices will be held for review.”\n\nHer breath caught.\n\n“And if I don’t agree,” she whispered.\n\nThe suit’s voice stayed gentle.\n\n“Then we reclassify what you did,” he said. “And it becomes criminal.”\n\nSilence.\n\nShe understood then: there was no path through this room that didn’t lead where they wanted.\n\nThe suit slid a pen toward her.\n\n“Sign,” he said.\n\nHer hand hovered.\n\nHer conscience screamed.\n\nHer fear answered louder.\n\nShe signed.\n\nThe suit took the paper, nodded with practiced satisfaction.\n\n“Good,” he said. “Now. One more thing.”\n\nShe looked up.\n\nThe suit’s eyes sharpened.\n\n“You will not contact anyone,” he said. “You will not post anything. You will not speak to press.”\n\nHe paused.\n\n“And you will forget,” he said softly, “that you ever saw a ring finger tap.”\n\nHer stomach turned.\n\nShe stood slowly on legs that didn’t feel like hers.\n\nThe suit smiled again.\n\n“We’ll drive you home,” he said.\n\nShe blinked.\n\n“I can walk.”\n\nThe suit’s smile didn’t change.\n\n“No,” he said. “You can’t.”\n\n⸻\n\nThat afternoon, her name vanished.\n\nHer accounts logged out everywhere.\nHer badge didn’t work.\nHer email bounced.\n\nEven her friends’ messages to her began to fail—sent, but never delivered.\n\nIt wasn’t a dramatic disappearance.\n\nIt was worse.\n\nA soft deletion.\n\nAnd in the room with the lamp and the Haitian radio humming low, One got a message from the librarian contact:\n\nRaw file received. Source went dark immediately after. Likely compromised.\n\nSandi’s face went pale.\n\n“We have to protect her,” she said.\n\nOne stared at the message.\n\nHe felt the familiar fire rise—the desire to react, to rage, to burn the feed down with truth.\n\nThen he remembered the rule.\n\nDon’t chase the feed.\n\nBuild the test.\n\nHe looked at Sandi.\n\n“We can’t save her with noise,” he said.\n\nSandi’s eyes hardened.\n\n“Then how.”\n\nOne’s voice was steady.\n\n“We make her disappearance visible without naming her,” he said. “We force the regime to choose: admit they control edits… or admit they punish witnesses.”\n\nSandi exhaled sharply.\n\n“Release the raw,” she said.\n\nOne nodded.\n\n“But strategically,” he replied. “So it protects her, not exposes her.”\n\n⸻\n\n[Scene extracted → canon/SCENE_31_THE_COUNTERMOVE.md]",
