       "body": "Scene: Carmichael Alone\n(Immediately Following the Interrupted Meeting)\nLocation: The same private study. The door has closed. He is alone.\n\nThe silence was louder than the argument.\nCarmichael stood at the window for a long time, watching the taillights of the car disappear down the long driveway. Secret Service. His daughter. The man whose mother he'd just called illegal.\nHis grandchild.\nThe word felt foreign in his mouth, like a language he'd studied but never spoken.\nHe walked back to the desk and picked up the folder—the one with One's mother's name, her immigration status, the timeline of her illegal entry, her decades of off-the-books work.\nHe'd ordered the file months ago.\nJust in case.\nJust in case what? he thought now.\nIn case I needed ammunition against my own daughter's choices?\nHe set it down like it burned.\n\nThe phone on the desk buzzed again. He glanced at the screen:\nChief of Staff: \"Sir, networks are asking for comment on the debate refusal. Need guidance.\"\nHe didn't reply.\nAnother buzz:\nSenior Advisor: \"Polling shows the 'empty podium' strategy is working. Push harder?\"\nHe turned the phone face-down.\nAnother buzz. This one from a private number.\nHe picked it up.\nUnknown: \"She chose him. You know what that means.\"\nNo signature. He didn't need one.\nHis own people, already calculating whether Sandi was now a liability or an asset.\nHe threw the phone across the room.\nIt hit the wall and clattered to the floor, screen cracked but still glowing.\n\nHe poured himself another drink.\nThis time he drank it.\nThe bourbon burned, but not enough.\nHe thought about his father—dead twenty years now, but still louder in his head than most living people.\nHis father had said:\n\"You build a wall to keep the chaos out. You don't apologize for it. You don't explain it. You just build it higher when they try to climb over.\"\nCarmichael had built that wall his entire life.\nAnd tonight, his daughter had walked through it like it was made of smoke.\n\nHe sat down at the desk and opened his laptop.\nTyped into the search bar, then stopped.\nHis fingers hovered over the keys.\nWhat was he even looking for?\n\"How to reconcile with a daughter who thinks you're a racist\"?\n\"What to do when your grandchild has the heritage you spent a campaign demonizing\"?\n\"How to admit you were wrong without losing everything you've built\"?\nHe deleted the search bar text and closed the laptop.\n\nA memory surfaced, unbidden:\nSandi, maybe six years old, sitting on his lap in the campaign bus.\nShe'd asked him: \"Daddy, why are people so mad at you?\"\nAnd he'd said, without thinking:\n\"Because I'm trying to protect them, and they don't understand yet.\"\nShe'd nodded, trusting.\n\"Okay. I understand.\"\nHe'd kissed the top of her head and thought:\nThis is why I do it. For her. For her future.\nBut what future had he actually built?\nOne where she had to choose between him and the man she loved.\nOne where his grandchild would grow up knowing their grandfather had spent years on television calling their heritage a threat.\nOne where \"protection\" had become a cage.\n\nHe stood and walked to the bookshelf.\nFound the family photo album buried behind policy briefs and donor lists.\nFlipped it open.\nSandi at eight, gap-toothed, holding a trophy from her first debate competition.\nSandi at twelve, rolling her eyes at a campaign rally, bored but obedient.\nSandi at sixteen, standing stiff in a formal dress, smiling for cameras, eyes hollow.\nSandi at twenty-two, college graduation, standing between him and her mother, looking like she wanted to be anywhere else.\nHe closed the album.\nWhen had she stopped looking at him like he was her father?\nWhen had he stopped being one?\n\nHis phone buzzed again from the floor, screen still cracked.\nHe picked it up.\nSandi: \"I meant what I said. But I still love you. That's the part that hurts.\"\nHe stared at the message for a full minute.\nTyped:\n\"I—\"\nDeleted it.\nTyped:\n\"You don't understand what I—\"\nDeleted it.\nTyped:\n\"I love you too.\"\nHis thumb hovered over Send.\nThen he thought:\nWhat if she asks me to apologize?\nWhat if she asks me to admit I was wrong about Haitian migrants?\nWhat if she asks me to choose between her and the base that elected me?\nHe couldn't do that.\nCould he?\nHe locked the phone without sending anything.\n\nHe walked back to the window.\nThe city glowed.\nSomewhere out there, people were going to bed believing he was still in control.\nBelieving he was strong.\nBelieving he was right.\nHe'd spent his entire life building that belief.\nAnd tonight, his daughter had looked him in the eye and said:\n\"You've already lost me.\"\nHe realized, with cold clarity, that she was right.\nNot because she'd walked out.\nBut because he'd built a fortress so high\nthat when she'd needed him to be her father instead of a President,\nhe hadn't known how to climb down.\n\nHe picked up the folder again.\nStared at One's mother's name.\nMarie-Claude Baptiste.\nShe'd crossed the water twice.\nSurvived a dictator. Survived an earthquake.\nRaised a son who'd become his greatest political threat.\nAnd he'd called her illegal.\nHe'd called her people invaders.\nHe'd said they brought disease, chaos, disrespect.\nAnd now his grandchild would carry her blood.\nHe set the folder down and pressed his palms against his eyes.\nFor the first time in thirty years,\nhe felt something crack.\nNot guilt, exactly.\nNot repentance.\nJust the terrifying recognition\nthat he might have been wrong\nabout the one thing that mattered.\n\nHe opened his laptop again.\nTyped slowly:\nDraft: Private Message to Sandi\n\"I don't know how to fix this. I don't know if I can. But I need you to know—\"\nHe stopped.\nNeed you to know what?\nThat he loved her?\nShe already knew that.\nThat he was sorry?\nHe wasn't sure he was.\nThat he'd change?\nHe didn't know if he could.\nHe closed the laptop.\nWalked to the door.\nOpened it.\nHis Chief of Staff was waiting in the hall.\n\"Sir, we need a statement on—\"\n\"Not now,\" Carmichael said.\n\"Sir, the press is—\"\n\"I said not now.\"\nHe closed the door in the man's face.\nLocked it.\nWalked back to the center of the room.\nAnd for the first time in decades,\nPresident Carmichael sat down on the floor\nwith his back against the desk\nand allowed himself to feel\nthe full weight of what he'd just lost.\n\nEnd Scene: Carmichael Alone.\n\nThis is the record of the night\na man who believed himself anointed\nsat alone in a room\nand realized\nthat the throne he'd fought so hard to keep\nhad cost him the only person\nwho'd ever loved him\nwithout needing him to be powerful first.",
       "isHead": true
